#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-reconcile — make the board match reality, and say so when it cannot.

The board (~/.cc/boards/<repo>.json) is a file sessions write between turns; the box is what is actually
true. They drift: a worker is killed and its track stays `running` for days, a PR merges and its track stays
`review`, a worktree is deleted under a live entry. Everything downstream — `cc digest`, the Slack Home tab,
the owner's idea of what the box is doing — reads the board, so that drift is exactly what the owner sees.

  cc-reconcile [repo …]              report the drift, change nothing
  cc-reconcile --apply               …and fix what is mechanically decidable, and STOP a runaway
  cc-reconcile --apply --notify      …and push what is NOT to the owner (once a day per finding)
  cc-reconcile snapshot [repo …]     print the state snapshot and WRITE NOTHING — no file, no ledger, no sweep, no network
  cc-reconcile view                  the snapshot file as `cc ls` and cc-digest take it: TSV repo/track · state · waiting_on · why; no rows when missing or stale
  cc-reconcile selfcheck             the decision table + one end-to-end run, no network
    also: --json · -q · --no-net (never ask GitHub) · --grace SEC (default 600) · --repeat SEC (default 86400)
          --no-stop (report a runaway, never end it)

TWO KINDS OF DRIFT, and telling them apart is the whole job. A DECIDABLE one has exactly one right answer in
the evidence — the PR merged, so the track is merged — and is applied. An UNDECIDABLE one needs a person — a
PR closed without merging, a worktree gone, work that never committed — and is reported, never guessed at.
This tool only ever writes a track's status (through cc-board, so the lock is respected) and a note saying it
did. It deletes nothing, opens nothing, merges nothing.

A FINISHED ROW IS NEITHER KIND — it is not drift at all, and that is what kept being missed. `merged` and `done`
mean the work landed: cc-land deleted the branch on merge, so a gone branch, a gone worktree and a leftover
`.cc/push.err` are the shape of success, and none of the three is raised about such a row on suspicion alone. It
went out as "<repo>/land-queue-retry — its branch is not pushed" about a PR that had MERGED, and 88 of that
board's 98 rows are in that state. A row that is genuinely unpushed and NOT merged still says so.

WHAT A FINISHED ROW IS STILL TOLD — the silence above is for what the box CANNOT TELL, never for what it KNOWS.
A merged row whose branch really does hold commits origin lacks is still reported, because nothing downstream
would catch it: committed work is not uncommitted work, so cc-janitor rule 1 reads that worktree as clean and
removes it, branch and all. Forgiving a fact is how work gets deleted; only the doubt is forgiven (review #203).

WHAT COUNTS AS ALIVE — the one fact a file cannot hold, and the one this leans on hardest before demoting
anything: a `claude` whose cwd is the track's worktree (the Home tab's rule), a `cc-loop <repo> <track>`
(alive for hours with no claude under it while it waits out a usage limit), or a live pane in the track's
tmux window. Any of the three and the track is alive, whatever the board says. Missing that would turn
this into a tool that stops work instead of describing it — hence also --grace, below.

…AND A SUBAGENT IS NONE OF THE THREE. Since 2026-09-04 a backlog row is normally worked by a planning session's
own subagents: no worker, no cc-loop, no window of the track's own, so all three tests above see an empty
worktree and the row was on the owner's NEEDS YOU list ten minutes later. The session says so on the row —
`cc board set <repo> <track> agent me` writes its own tmux window there — and that mark counts as alive on two
tests and no fewer: an interactive `claude` is under that window (a pane left with a `tail -f` in it is not a
session), and the window is named for the row's own repo (a session speaks for its repo and no other). It claims
nothing else: the moment the session is gone the row is unattended again and is reported like any other, and a
mark made before the START of a usage limit that has since lifted is cold at any age — the subagent holding it died
on the 429 (see mark_why). cc-pulse reads the same mark on the same bounds and stops offering the row.

ALIVE IS NOT WORKING, though, and that is the third word. A live pane with nobody typing at it for 2 h is `idle`,
not `running`: a track sat under 🏃 for 1 d 19 h because a session was parked at its prompt long after its headless
loop had been aborted (owner, 2026-09-01). A worker or a `cc-loop` alive means work in flight whatever the keyboard
shows, so neither is ever called idle, and a declared question outranks both. See idle_session().

WHAT COUNTS AS WAITING, which is NOT the opposite of alive. `running` used to mean "a process exists", so five
sessions that had each stopped to ask the owner a question were indexed as running for four hours and reached no
list of his (owner, 2026-08-31: "ok fix"). The question is not whether a pid is there — all five were — but
whether anything happens WITHOUT HIM. A worker's own `STATUS: BLOCKED: <question>` line is the declaration, and
it outranks presence: that track is `waiting`, and it is an `ask` for as long as the question stands. A loop
sitting out a usage limit is waiting on the CLOCK, gets its own row and never reaches him. A worker between
iterations has declared nothing and is simply running. See waits_for(); nothing is ever inferred from prose.

THE SNAPSHOT (~/.cc/state/reconcile.json, review rec #7). Every unfiltered run writes what it just decided —
effective state, live presence, who each track waits on and why. This tool is the sole decider; `cc-digest` and
`cc ls` read it through `view` (one TSV row per track) rather than re-deriving any of it; missing or stale, the board's word stands.
THE HOME TAB AND cc-pulse TAKE A FRESH ONE INSTEAD (arch review 2026-09-08, rec 8): each pass runs `snapshot` once
and reads state, live, waiting_on and why off it — neither keeps a state, blocked-reason or liveness ladder of its own
any more, so every consumer says the same thing about the same box. `snapshot` is pure for that reason: it decides
and prints, and touches neither the file above nor the wedged ledger nor the sweeps below, so a tab refresh or a
pulse tick can never move the timer's state. A consumer that gets no snapshot keeps the board's word and claims nothing
alive — the same fallback `view` gives.

THE ONE RUNAWAY cc-loop CANNOT CATCH is its own. cc-loop judges a loop that is looping — three iterations in a
row with nothing to show and it stops itself. A loop that is WEDGED never finishes an iteration, so it never
reaches that judgement, and an ORPHANED worker has no loop above it at all (killing a loop's window re-parents
its `claude` to systemd, still spending, still editing the worktree). Both are alive, so everything else here
reads them as healthy. Two facts settle them, and both are cheap:
  · orphan — a headless `claude` in a track worktree with no `cc-loop <repo> <track>` anywhere. Nothing will
    time it out, reap it, or commit a line of what it writes. Ended on sight.
  · wedged — a worker alive with the track's journal, loop log and worktree all untouched for 3 h. cc-loop caps
    an iteration at 1 h and logs both ends, so three missed beats is not slow, it is stuck. Ended only after
    TWO runs 20 min apart both saw it, and never while a usage limit is standing — that is a loop waiting, not
    a loop wedged.
A THIRD SHAPE, and neither of the two above: a track that FINISHED, opened its PR, and nobody landed it.
Board and box agree — `review`, PR open — so no drift fires and nothing here ever said a word, while four
finished PRs sat until the owner asked (2026-09-01). It is not a person's decision, it is one command, so
the repo's live session is offered it ONCE per PR (and once more if it falls out of a lane unlanded); a row
the board says is `waiting` — a person's word — and a PR already being landed (a live landing argv naming it,
or a job under ~/.cc/state/land/) are skipped; only once it has stood past CC_PENDING_ALERT (default 4 h)
does it become the owner's, as one #alerts line per window.

Ending one means SIGTERM to its process group, `blocked` on the board, and one line to the owner with the
numbers. There is no spend cap anywhere in this box (owner, 2026-08-31: "no spend cap as some just are
expensive"): an expensive job that keeps moving is never touched by any of this.

THE FAILURE INVESTIGATION rides the same turn (docs/design-failure-noticing-2026-09-11.md, The investigation). Once
per unfiltered --apply pass: if ~/.cc/config has CC_FAILURES_INVESTIGATE=on — the owner's one named setting, and
absent it is inert on every box — and `cc-tier allows explore` says yes, and no `cc-audit failure` is running, the
pass takes ONE eligible cluster with `cc-failures claim` (once per cluster, two a day; the ledger says why not) and
launches `cc-audit failure <claim>` as a transient user unit that outlives this oneshot (a detached child without a
user manager). The tier saying no stands the pass down and claims nothing: the cluster stays eligible and visible
with its age on `cc board failures`. Recording is never gated; the launch is the only thing the tier holds. Nothing
here reads the report, opens a row or posts: cc-audit delivers, and a finding cannot make work. See investigate().
CC_RECONCILE_LAUNCH=CMD (tests) runs `CMD failure <claim>` in place of the unit and skips the running-investigation
scan, which is box-wide: a fixture must never be held by the box's own investigation.

Exit: 0 the board matches reality · 1 something needs a person · 2 usage error.
"""
import calendar, glob, json, os, re, shutil, signal, subprocess, sys, time

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
BIN = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))      # siblings come from the same bin/ as this copy: a worktree tests itself
BOARDS, WTS, STATE = f"{HOME}/.cc/boards", f"{HOME}/.cc/worktrees", f"{HOME}/.cc/state"
GRACE = 600        # a track the board touched less than this ago is left alone: `cc --go` writes the board, then claude takes a moment to appear
REPEAT = 86400     # a finding that persists is worth one push a day, not one every timer tick
PRMAX = 40         # PR lookups per run — a timer job must never stall on the network
ORPHANPRS = 10     # …and the same cap for the branch→PR lookups orphans() makes: past it a worktree is reported as it always was
WEDGED = 3 * 3600  # a worker alive with nothing moved for this long: cc-loop caps one iteration at 1 h and logs both ends
IDLE = 2 * 3600    # a live pane with nobody typing at it for this long: open is not working (owner, 2026-09-01)
AGENT_MARK = 6 * 3600   # …and the same rule for an `agent` mark: it holds a row for this long, then goes cold
WEDGE_SEEN = 5400  # …and only if the previous run saw it too, which on a 20-min timer is one strike, then another
PENDING = 14400    # finished with an open PR for this long and nobody landing it: the session was told 12 times already
BETWEEN_ROUNDS = 1800   # a landing is ROUNDS with gaps between them — a repair to dispatch, a spend decision handed
                        # back, a re-queue — and only a round holds a job file. A PR whose landing record was written
                        # less ago than this is between rounds, not abandoned: PR #421, 2026-09-11, was nagged by the
                        # 10:51Z tick, which fell in the six minutes between one round ending and the next being queued
TERMINAL = ("merged", "done")
FRESH = 3600       # a snapshot older than this (~3 ticks of the 20-min timer) decides nothing: `view` prints no rows and its readers keep the board's word
QUEUED = ("queued", "todo")   # the board says `queued` (one vocabulary with the App Home tab); boards written before
                              # that change, and a hand that still types `todo`, mean both words must be READ here


def now():
    return time.time()


def epoch(ts):
    """Board timestamps are UTC '2026-08-30T21:01:43Z'; anything else is 'long ago', never 'in the future'."""
    try:
        return float(calendar.timegm(time.strptime(str(ts), "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")))
    except Exception:
        return 0.0


def mark_why(t, wins, repo, t0, mark, started=0.0):
    """The `agent` mark, weighed: (does it hold, and if not, what to say). A mark is a SESSION'S WINDOW and the
    time it was made, and either half can be worth nothing — the window can be gone or belong to another repo,
    and the mark itself goes cold.

    ALIVE IS NOT WORKING, which is this file's oldest lesson (owner, 2026-09-01, after a session sat open and
    idle for 1 d 19 h). A window merely existing held a row open for as long as the session lived: mark a row,
    let the subagent die or simply move on without clearing it, and the row read `running` for ever — no grace
    rule, no missing-worktree ask, and cc-pulse never offering it again. idle_session() cannot reach it either,
    because a marked row has no window of its own to be idle. So the mark expires. A session still on the row
    says so by marking again, which costs it one command; one that moved on says nothing, and the row comes
    back (review of #242).

    A USAGE LIMIT ENDS A MARK BEFORE ITS TIME — decided here once; cc-pulse reads the snapshot. On 2026-09-05 a
    limit at 09:05Z killed a planning session's subagents mid-row; their marks stayed warm until 15:05Z, four
    hours after the limit lifted. `started` is when the last episode cc-limit came back from BEGAN, and a mark
    that old was made before the 429 that killed the subagent holding it. The START and not the reset: the box
    does not stop for the episode — cc-model moves every session to the fallback about a minute in — so a mark
    made after that is a subagent that is alive and working (review of #264). A mark AT the start second is cold
    too, the same way a mark exactly `mark` old is: that second is the one the limit was first seen in."""
    ag = (t.get("agent") or "").strip()
    # AN ORCHESTRATOR SPEAKS FOR ITS REPO TOO. A session a person steers is in a window named `<repo>`; an orch
    # beside it, in the same checkout and on the same board, is `<repo>@<alias>`. Read as plain equality, every
    # mark an orch made was "never this repo's to make", so its rows were unattended from the first tick — and
    # a handoff that carried marks to an orch successor carried them somewhere they stopped counting.
    win = wins.get(ag) or ""
    if not ag or not (win == repo or win.startswith(repo + "@")):
        return False, ""                # no mark, or one that was never this repo's to make: see facts()
    at = epoch(t.get("agent_at"))
    if not at:
        return False, "marked by a live session, but the mark carries no time"   # written before cc-board stamped it
    if t0 - at >= mark:
        return False, f"marked by a live session {(t0 - at) / 3600:.0f} h ago, mark expired"
    if started and at <= started:
        return False, ("marked before the usage limit that began at "
                       + time.strftime("%H:%MZ", time.gmtime(started)) + " — that subagent died with it")
    return True, ""


# ── reality ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def procs():
    """One pass over /proc → (live tracks, {ppids}, {pids holding a session}, {track: [worker pids]},
    {track: [loop pids]}). Two signals, one walk: a `claude` sitting in a track worktree (the same rule the Home
    tab uses) and a `cc-loop <repo> <track>`, which is the only thing alive while a loop waits out a usage limit.
    The ppid set is what makes a tmux pane 'live' below. A WORKER is a headless claude — the budget flag is on the
    command line of every one of those and of no interactive session, which is exactly how `cc … --go` tells them
    apart.

    THE THIRD SET IS EVERY PID WITH AN INTERACTIVE `claude` UNDER IT, ancestors and all, and it is a stricter
    question than "does this pane have a child": a window somebody left a `tail -f` or an `htop` in after the
    session exited has a child for ever, and an `agent` mark on such a window would keep a row alive for ever with
    it. cc-pulse asks the same question of a pane (live_claude) before it types into one; this is that rule, taken
    off the walk this already pays for."""
    live, ppids, root = set(), set(), WTS + os.sep
    workers, loops, parent, inter, sessions = {}, {}, {}, set(), set()
    for pid in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not pid.isdigit():
            continue
        try:
            with open(f"/proc/{pid}/status") as f:
                for ln in f:
                    if ln.startswith("PPid:"):
                        ppids.add(ln.split()[1])
                        parent[pid] = ln.split()[1]
                        break
            argv = open(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline", "rb").read().decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\0")
            comm = open(f"/proc/{pid}/comm").read().strip()
        except OSError:
            continue                                    # gone between listdir and here, or not ours to read
        for i, a in enumerate(argv):                    # `cc-loop <repo> <track> …`, however the shebang re-wrote argv[0]
            if a and os.path.basename(a) == "cc-loop" and len(argv) > i + 2 and argv[i + 1] and argv[i + 2]:
                live.add(f"{argv[i + 1]}/{argv[i + 2]}")
                loops.setdefault(f"{argv[i + 1]}/{argv[i + 2]}", []).append(int(pid))
                break
        if comm == "claude":
            if not any(a in ("-p", "--print") for a in argv):
                inter.add(pid)                          # an INTERACTIVE claude: a prompt somebody could type at
            try:
                cwd = os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/cwd")
            except OSError:
                continue
            if cwd.startswith(root):
                key = "/".join(cwd[len(root):].split(os.sep)[:2])
                live.add(key)
                if any(a.startswith("--max-budget-usd") for a in argv):
                    workers.setdefault(key, []).append(int(pid))
    for pid in inter:                                   # …and everything ABOVE one is holding that session, up to
        up, seen = pid, set()                           # init. A second pass, because `parent` is only whole now.
        while up and up not in seen and up != "1":
            seen.add(up)
            sessions.add(up)
            up = parent.get(up, "")
    return live, ppids, sessions, workers, loops


def idle_for(repo, track, wt):
    """Seconds since the track last MOVED. Three places, because each one alone lies: the journal is written
    once an iteration, the loop log twice, and the worktree only when there was code to show for it."""
    newest = 0.0
    st = f"{STATE}/{repo}/{track}"
    for path in (f"{st}/progress.md", f"{st}/loop.log", f"{st}/runs", f"{wt}/.git"):
        try:
            newest = max(newest, os.stat(path).st_mtime)
        except OSError:
            pass
    try:                                                # a worktree's .git is a FILE pointing at the real gitdir
        gd = open(f"{wt}/.git").read().strip()
        if gd.startswith("gitdir:"):
            newest = max(newest, os.stat(os.path.join(gd[7:].strip(), "HEAD")).st_mtime)
    except OSError:
        pass
    return max(0.0, now() - newest) if newest else 0.0  # nothing to read is not evidence of being stuck


def limit_stamp():
    """cc-limit's stamp as (until, observers). "<until>\t<how>\t<first-seen>\t<model>\t<observers>", split on
    TAB because field 4 may be empty. The observers are the distinct RUNS that hit it, and cc-limit and cc-model
    call it the box's at two (one run's limit is not the box's, 2026-09-04): below that it holds no track here."""
    try:
        f = open(os.environ.get("CC_LIMIT_STAMP") or f"{STATE}/claude-limit").read().split("\n")[0].split("\t")
        obs = [k for k in (f[4].split(",") if len(f) > 4 else [])
               if len(k) == 8 and all(c in "0123456789abcdef" for c in k)]
        return float(f[0]), len(obs)
    except Exception:
        return 0.0, 0


def limit_active():
    """A Claude usage limit standing over the BOX: two runs hit it. cc-limit owns the stamp; a loop waiting one
    out is not wedged. A stamp one run hit binds that run alone and says nothing about any other track (#215)."""
    until, n = limit_stamp()
    return until > now() and n >= 2


def limit_started():
    """When the last usage-limit episode cc-limit resumed from BEGAN (epoch), or 0. `cc-limit resume` writes it —
    "<started>\t<reset>\t<resumed>", see WHAT ELSE THE LIMIT KILLED there. The START, not the reset: the box goes
    on working on the fallback for the rest of an episode, so only a mark older than the 429 itself belongs to a
    subagent that died (review of #264). Field 2 is the reset, and one still ahead says nothing — only an episode
    that is over tells you who did not survive it."""
    try:
        f = open(os.environ.get("CC_LIMIT_RESUMED") or f"{STATE}/limit-resumed").read().split("\n")[0].split("\t")
        if len(f) != 3:
            return 0.0          # not the three fields cc-limit writes: say nothing rather than read field 1 as a start
        at, until = float(f[0]), float(f[1])
        return at if 0 < at and 0 < until <= now() else 0.0
    except Exception:
        return 0.0


def limit_until():
    """When the standing box-wide limit lifts (epoch), or 0. The snapshot carries the hour so a consumer can say
    "held until 17:06" without re-reading cc-limit's stamp."""
    until, n = limit_stamp()
    return until if until > now() and n >= 2 else 0.0


def runaway(f, seen_before, wedged=WEDGED):
    """Pure — why this LIVE track is spending without working, or "". The orphan is unambiguous and immediate;
    the wedged one has to be seen twice, because "nothing moved" is also what a slow hour looks like."""
    if f.get("orphans"):
        return ("a headless worker with no cc-loop above it — nothing will time it out, reap it or commit "
                f"what it writes ({len(f['orphans'])} process(es), {f['idle'] / 3600:.1f} h since anything moved)")
    if f.get("workers") and not f.get("limited") and f["idle"] >= wedged and seen_before:
        return (f"its loop is wedged: alive with nothing moved for {f['idle'] / 3600:.1f} h — no commit, no "
                "journal line, no iteration, and no usage limit to be waiting on")
    return ""


def waits_for(f):
    """Pure — ("person"|"clock"|"", why): who this track is waiting on. THE ROW THIS TOOL WAS MISSING. Five course
    sessions sat four hours with a question for the owner while everything here said `running`, because every rule
    below asks "is a process alive" and all five were. Alive is not the question. The question is whether anything
    happens WITHOUT HIM.

    A PERSON, and only because the worker SAID SO. The one thing that declares it is the worker's own
    `STATUS: BLOCKED: <question>` line — the box's sender-decides protocol, the same line the Home tab reads. Journal
    PROSE is never read for this: "waiting for the syllabus" in a sentence is not a declaration, and a NEEDS YOU row
    the owner cannot act on costs more than the row we leave out. A declaration OUTRANKS a live process, because a
    worker that wrote it has stopped whatever its pid is still doing.

    THE CLOCK IS NOT A PERSON. A `cc-loop` sitting out a Claude usage limit resumes by itself at a known hour; telling
    the owner to act on it is telling him to do nothing. It is its own row, with its own word, and it never reaches
    NEEDS YOU. Narrow on purpose — a live loop with no worker under it, while a limit stands — so that a track merely
    stopped for some other reason is not quietly filed under the clock."""
    jk, jt = f.get("journal") or (None, "")
    if jk == "BLOCKED":
        return ("person", jt or "it stood down without saying what it needs")
    if f.get("limited") and f.get("loops") and not f.get("workers"):
        return ("clock", "a Claude usage limit — the loop resumes by itself, nobody is being asked")
    return ("", "")


def panes(lines, ppids, sessions=(), t0=None):
    """Pure — the whole reading of a `tmux list-panes` listing, so the selfcheck can drive it with no tmux server:
    (tracks whose window has something running in it, {track: seconds since that window last moved},
    {mark: window name} for every window a SESSION is in). A window alone proves nothing — `cc` leaves a dead one
    behind an exited session (`exec bash`) — so a track's window must have a child in its pane, exactly as `cc`
    decides it (pane_live).

    THE IDLE CLOCK IS `#{window_activity}`, the same field cc-secretary reads for the same question. An open
    session is not a working one: the owner watched a track sit in RUNNING for 1 d 19 h because a `claude` was at
    its prompt with nobody typing at it (2026-09-01). See idle_session().

    THE THIRD MAP IS STRICTER THAN THE FIRST, and it is not just the track windows. It is what an `agent` mark may
    name (cc-board `agent_id`), and a mark is a claim that a SESSION is there — so a pane with any child at all is
    not enough: a window somebody left a `tail -f` in after the session exited has one for ever, and the row would
    never be reported again. procs() answers the real question, an interactive `claude` under that pane, which is
    also what cc-pulse asks (live_claude) before it types into one. The NAME comes back with it because a session
    may only speak for its own repo — see facts()."""
    live, idle, wins, t0 = set(), {}, {}, t0 if t0 is not None else now()
    for ln in lines:
        f = ln.split("\t")
        if len(f) < 2 or f[1].strip() not in ppids:
            continue                                    # nothing running in that pane: the window is a leftover
        if len(f) > 3 and f[3].strip() and f[1].strip() in sessions:
            wins[f[3].strip()] = f[0]                   # "<window id>:<tmux server pid>" — cc-board writes both
        if f[0].count("/") != 1:
            continue                                    # a TRACK window is `<repo>/<track>`; a session's is not
        live.add(f[0])
        try:                                            # a window with no activity stamp is not evidence of idleness
            act = float(f[2]) if len(f) > 2 and f[2].strip() else 0.0
        except ValueError:
            act = 0.0
        if act:
            was = max(0.0, t0 - act)                    # a window last moved when its BUSIEST pane did
            idle[f[0]] = min(idle[f[0]], was) if f[0] in idle else was
    return live, idle, wins


def tmux_live(ppids, sessions=()):
    """panes() over the real listing; nothing at all when there is no tmux server to ask."""
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["tmux", "list-panes", "-a", "-F",
                              "#{window_name}\t#{pane_pid}\t#{window_activity}\t#{window_id}:#{pid}"],
                             capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
    except Exception:
        return set(), {}, {}
    return panes(out.stdout.splitlines(), ppids, sessions)


def journal(repo, track):
    """(kind, text) from the LAST STATUS line a worker left — the box's own protocol for 'I finished' and
    'I need you'. A fresh dispatch appends after it, so only the last line speaks."""
    try:
        body = open(f"{STATE}/{repo}/{track}/progress.md", errors="replace").read().splitlines()[-400:]
    except OSError:
        return (None, "")
    for ln in reversed(body):
        s = ln.strip()
        if s.upper().startswith("STATUS: DONE"):
            return ("DONE", "")
        if s.upper().startswith("STATUS: BLOCKED"):
            return ("BLOCKED", s[len("STATUS: BLOCKED"):].lstrip(": ").strip())
    return (None, "")


def loop_end(repo, track):
    """cc-loop's own last word ('exit 4: No commits or journal change…'), but only if it came AFTER the last
    iteration it started — a loop killed mid-iteration ended with no word at all, and that is a finding."""
    try:
        body = open(f"{STATE}/{repo}/{track}/loop.log", errors="replace").read().splitlines()[-200:]
    except OSError:
        return ""
    for ln in reversed(body):
        m = re.search(r"\bexit (\d+): (.+)", ln)
        if m:
            return f"exit {m.group(1)}: {m.group(2)}"[:160]
        if re.search(r"\biter \d+/\d+ start", ln):
            return ""
    return ""


def pr_state(repo, url, net=True):
    """MERGED / OPEN / CLOSED, '?' when the answer could not be had (no network, no gh, no such PR), None when
    we never asked. '?' is reported, never acted on: an unreachable GitHub is not evidence about a track."""
    if not net or not url or not shutil.which("gh"):
        return None if not net else "?"
    cwd = f"{HOME}/dev/{repo}"
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["gh", "pr", "view", str(url), "--json", "state", "-q", ".state"],
                           cwd=cwd if os.path.isdir(cwd) else None, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
    except Exception:
        return "?"
    s = p.stdout.strip().upper()
    return s if s in ("MERGED", "OPEN", "CLOSED") else "?"


def branch_prs(repo, branch, net=True):
    """[(number, STATE, merged_at)] for the PRs opened FROM <branch>, newest first — the other direction from
    pr_state(), which starts at a PR a board row already names. `merged_at` is epoch seconds for a MERGED one and
    0.0 for every other state, because WHEN it merged is the difference between a delivery being cleaned up and a
    leftover (see orphans()). None when the answer could not be had (no network, no gh, no checkout to ask from, a
    call that failed), and None is not "there is no PR": it is no evidence, and the caller falls back to what it
    can decide without GitHub. Asked from the repo's own checkout — `gh pr list --head` with no repo in front of
    it reads whatever repo the cwd belongs to, which would answer about the wrong project."""
    cwd = f"{HOME}/dev/{repo}"
    if not net or not branch or not shutil.which("gh") or not os.path.isdir(cwd):
        return None
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["gh", "pr", "list", "--head", branch, "--state", "all", "--limit", "10",
                            "--json", "number,state,mergedAt"], cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
        if p.returncode:
            return None
        return [(int(r["number"]), str(r.get("state") or "").upper(), epoch(r.get("mergedAt")))
                for r in json.loads(p.stdout or "[]")]
    except Exception:
        return None


REVIEW_MARK = "cc-land-review"      # the HTML comment cc-land writes a review down in; the verdict is a FIELD of it
# The two shapes it writes, ANCHORED AT THE START of the comment exactly as cc-land's own verdict_in() is: a
# finding that quotes a marker — reviewing anything that reviews, one does — is text about the change, not the
# answer to it, and matching anywhere let the quote win. The v2 shape carries the change key; the older one is
# keyed by the head alone and is still honoured.
VERDICT_RE = (re.compile(rf"\A\s*<!-- {re.escape(REVIEW_MARK)} v2 change=[0-9a-f]+[^>]*? head=([0-9a-f]+)"
                         r"[^>]*? verdict=(LAND-AFTER-FIX|DO-NOT-LAND|LAND)\b"),
              re.compile(rf"\A\s*<!-- {re.escape(REVIEW_MARK)} ([0-9a-f]{{12}}) "
                         r"(LAND-AFTER-FIX|DO-NOT-LAND|LAND) -->"))
STOPPING = ("DO-NOT-LAND", "LAND-AFTER-FIX")   # the two verdicts cc-land refuses to merge on, a red gate's equal


def refused(repo, url, net=True):
    """The stopping verdict already recorded on <url>'s CURRENT head — "DO-NOT-LAND" / "LAND-AFTER-FIX" — or "".
    "" when there is none AND when GitHub could not be asked: a verdict nobody can read is not a refusal, and this
    only ever silences a nag, so the doubt costs a wake-up and never a merge.

    WHY THE WATCHDOG ASKS AT ALL. "finished, PR open, not landed — review and land it" is the box reading a status
    and guessing at a next move, and it is the wrong move for a change the box has already read and said no to. PR
    #404 drew it on 2026-09-11 with DO-NOT-LAND recorded against its head twice — the landing's own re-review and
    a security read — while a replacement row was already building the fix: a wake-up and a turn to say no, and a
    less careful seat would have queued a refused change for deploy. `OPEN` was all this rule read, and a PR state
    is not a verdict.

    THE HEAD IS HALF THE ANSWER. A verdict is about a CHANGE, so the push that answers it spends it: once the head
    moves the PR is unread again and "land it" is the right offer. Only a marker written about the head the PR is
    at NOW counts, and of those the LAST stands — cc-land lets a stricter verdict be written over one that already
    does, so the newest is the one in force.

    Asked only about a row that is otherwise about to be nagged, which is a handful a run at most."""
    if not net or not url or not shutil.which("gh"):
        return ""
    cwd = f"{HOME}/dev/{repo}"
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["gh", "pr", "view", str(url), "--json", "headRefOid,comments"],
                           cwd=cwd if os.path.isdir(cwd) else None, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
        if p.returncode:
            return ""
        j = json.loads(p.stdout or "{}")
        head = str(j.get("headRefOid") or "")
        if not head:
            return ""
        out = ""
        for c in j.get("comments") or []:
            m = next((x for x in (r.match(str(c.get("body") or "")) for r in VERDICT_RE) if x), None)
            if m and head.startswith(m.group(1)):
                out = m.group(2)                    # the last marker about this head is the one that stands
        return out if out in STOPPING else ""
    except Exception:
        return ""


def alert(msg):
    """#alerts, best-effort: a timer job must never fail on Slack being down. Under a harness
    (CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY, the switch every suite sets) it goes to the log, not the real channel."""
    if os.environ.get("CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY"):
        try:
            with open(os.environ.get("CC_NOTIFY_LOG") or f"{STATE}/notify.log", "a") as fh:
                fh.write(f"alerts\t{msg}\n")
        except OSError:
            pass
        return
    try:
        subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-slack", "post", "-c", "#alerts", msg], capture_output=True, timeout=30)
    except Exception:
        pass


def host_marker(repo, track, name, home=None):
    """Where the HOST's <name> marker for this track is — where the host WRITES, and the only place `push!` is read.
    ONE RULE, and every reader follows it: `cc ls`, `cc digest`, the Slack Home tab and this tool. `commit!` reads
    this AND the member's own copy — see commit_marker.

    For a MEMBER WORKSPACE it is ~/.cc/push-state/<repo>/<track>/<name> and never <wt>/.cc/<name>. Every host-side
    cc-checkpoint run in a member's tree writes its state there (CC_CHECKPOINT_STATE_DIR, set by `cc done` and by
    the 5-minute sweep), because ~/.cc/worktrees/<repo> is bound read-WRITE in the member's live boundary and a link
    raced in at one of those names would be written through as the owner. So the files of those names inside the
    worktree are the MEMBER's, written and cleared by their own in-boundary hook and by nothing on the host.
    Read as the host's, they broke both ways: the member's push.err — their hook has no credential by design, that
    is why the host pushes for them, so it is rewritten every turn and never cleared — lit `push!` for ever and made
    this tool ask "its branch is not pushed" every 20 minutes about a branch the sweep had pushed (review round 10
    of #225); and once the host's own commit refusal moved to ~/.cc/push-state, a `cc done` that refused to commit a
    staged secret lit `commit!` nowhere at all (round 11). Every other repo on the box keeps its markers in the
    worktree, where the hook that writes them clears them."""
    h = home or HOME
    if os.path.exists(f"{h}/dev/{repo}/.cc/member-workspace"):
        return f"{h}/.cc/push-state/{repo}/{track}/{name}"
    return f"{h}/.cc/worktrees/{repo}/{track}/.cc/{name}"


def push_marker(repo, track, home=None):
    """The failed-push marker — the `push!` in cc ls / cc digest and push_err below. See host_marker."""
    return host_marker(repo, track, "push.err", home)


def commit_marker(repo, track, home=None):
    """The refused-checkpoint marker — the `commit!` in cc ls / cc digest and commit_err below. TWO PLACES, EITHER
    LIGHTS IT, and every reader does the same OR: the host's (host_marker) and, in a member workspace, the file the
    member's OWN in-boundary Stop hook writes at <wt>/.cc/checkpoint.err (cc-checkpoint's refuse()).

    Both halves refuse a commit and each can only write where it runs. Reading only the host's made a member's own
    refusal invisible everywhere — a session that stages .env.local is refused, nothing is committed, and cc ls, cc
    digest, the Home tab and this tool all said nothing, while the 5-minute sweep skipped the track because a refusal
    moves no HEAD (review round 12 of #225). push.err stays host-only: the member's is credential-less by design, so
    it is rewritten every turn and nothing clears it, and reading it nagged for ever about a pushed branch (round 10).

    The member's file is theirs, so only its EXISTENCE is read — never its content, anywhere a host reader would
    print or trust it. Returns whichever path exists, the host's first, and the host's when neither does."""
    h = home or HOME
    host = host_marker(repo, track, "checkpoint.err", h)
    member = f"{h}/.cc/worktrees/{repo}/{track}/.cc/checkpoint.err"    # == host outside a member workspace
    return next((p for p in (host, member) if os.path.exists(p)), host)


def wt_branch(wt):
    """The branch checked out in <wt>, or "" — a detached HEAD, a directory that is no checkout of its own, and a git
    that would not answer all say "", because none of them is a branch anything here can look up. The `.git` test is
    what keeps `git -C` from walking UP out of the directory and answering about some enclosing repo instead."""
    if not os.path.exists(f"{wt}/.git"):
        return ""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", wt, "symbolic-ref", "--short", "HEAD"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
    except Exception:
        return ""
    return "" if r.returncode else r.stdout.strip()


def branch_unpushed(wt):
    """Does this worktree really hold commits origin does not have? True yes · False no · None cannot tell.

    `.cc/push.err` is not that answer. cc-checkpoint clears the marker on its OWN next successful push, but nothing
    clears it when the branch gets to origin any other way — a person pushing by hand after a refused lease, the
    other writer's work merging — and `lease_refused` APPENDS to it without ever pushing. So one bad minute left a
    file that made this tool report `its branch is not pushed` about a branch that was pushed, every 20 minutes,
    for as long as the file lived. A push updates the remote-tracking ref whoever makes it, so HEAD reachable from
    `origin/<branch>` IS 'origin has this'. None on any doubt, and the caller then keeps the marker's answer."""
    def git(*a):
        try:
            return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", wt, *a], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
        except Exception:
            return None
    br = wt_branch(wt)
    if not br:
        return None                      # no git here, or a detached HEAD: nothing this rule can say
    up = git("rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", f"refs/remotes/origin/{br}")
    if not up or up.returncode:
        return None                      # the branch has never been on origin: nothing to compare it against
    anc = git("merge-base", "--is-ancestor", "HEAD", up.stdout.strip())
    return None if anc is None else anc.returncode != 0


def has_remote(repo):
    try:
        return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", f"{HOME}/dev/{repo}", "remote", "get-url", "origin"],
                              capture_output=True, timeout=10).returncode == 0
    except Exception:
        return False


# ── the decision ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def idle_session(f, idle=IDLE):
    """Pure — True when this track is LIVE ONLY because a session sits open at its prompt, and has for `idle`.

    OPEN IS NOT WORKING, and the tab had no word for the difference: a track showed as running for 1 d 19 h because a
    `claude` was parked at its prompt in the track's window, its headless loop having been aborted two days before
    (owner, 2026-09-01: "why is 🏃 subproj-carpet-rectify · 1 it still in running"). Narrow on purpose, and it is the
    narrowness that makes it safe:
      · a WORKER or a `cc-loop` alive is work in flight whatever the pane shows — a headless iteration can think for an
        hour without touching the window — so either one and this is False;
      · no activity stamp (tmux silent, no window, a fixture that carries none) reads as 0 and is never idleness;
      · it says nothing about WAITING. A worker's `STATUS: BLOCKED` outranks it in effective(), exactly as it outranks
        presence — a track that stopped to ask is the owner's, not a quiet one."""
    return bool(f.get("live")) and not f.get("workers") and not f.get("loops") and f.get("pane_idle", 0.0) >= idle


def decide(f):
    """Pure — facts in, verdicts out, so the selfcheck can drive every branch without a box around it.

    Returns [(kind, status|None, why)]:  fix = decidable, applied silently · ask = needs a person, applied
    when it also has one right status and always reported · info = could not tell, only printed."""
    st, pr, has_pr = f["status"], f.get("pr_state") or "", bool(f.get("pr"))
    # A SUBAGENT IS NOBODY TO /proc. Since 2026-09-04 the box's default execution model is a planning session doing
    # a board row's work in its own subagents: no worker, no cc-loop, no window of the track's own, so every test
    # this tool makes saw an empty worktree and, ten minutes past the grace, said "no worker, and nothing says why"
    # on the owner's NEEDS YOU list. `agent` is the row field that session sets (`cc board set <repo> <track> agent
    # me`, cc-board agent_id) and facts() has already asked whether the window it names still holds a session and
    # whether the mark is still warm — see mark_why(). It is the SESSION being alive that the mark claims, and
    # nothing more: the moment that window is gone, or the mark goes cold, the row is reconciled exactly as any
    # other unattended row is, and a cold mark says so in the reason rather than as "nothing says why".
    live = f["live"] or bool(f.get("agent_live"))
    jk = (f.get("journal") or (None, ""))[0]
    who, wwhy = waits_for(f)      # waits_for owns the question itself: this only needs to know DONE from not-DONE
    if (f.get("kind") or "track") == "session":
        # A SESSION row (cc-board KINDS) is a channel, not a task: alive is its normal state and it is never done, so
        # no rule below — presence, grace, PR, worktree, runaway — is drift for it (five were flipped to `running`
        # on every tick before this; owner, 2026-09-01). The ONE row it shares with a track is the owner's question,
        # the row waits_for() was written for: it is asked, set `waiting`, offered and paged exactly like a track's
        # (review of #132), and clears the same way, the moment the journal's newest STATUS is not BLOCKED.
        if who == "person" and st not in TERMINAL:
            return [("ask", "waiting", "it is waiting on you: " + wwhy)]
        if st == "waiting":
            # It has to leave `waiting` — that word is the owner's NEEDS YOU list, and an answered question must not
            # sit on it. But the word it goes back to is one the box can SEE: `running` is a claim that a session is
            # open, and a channel whose window is gone makes no such claim. The display side stopped printing it
            # (#174); this is the board field, which was still flipped to `running` on every tick of a dead session.
            return [("fix", "running" if f["live"] else "queued", "its question was answered")]
        return []
    out, settled = [], False
    if f.get("stop_why"):               # spending without producing: the one live track this tool ends rather than describes
        out.append(("stop", "blocked", f["stop_why"]))
        settled = True
    elif who == "person" and st not in TERMINAL:
        # ABOVE THE PRESENCE RULE ON PURPOSE. `waiting` is reported every run, not just the run that sets it: the
        # finding is "he is being waited on", and that stays true — and stays worth one push a day — for as long as
        # the question does. It goes back to `running` by itself the moment the journal's newest STATUS is not BLOCKED.
        out.append(("ask", "waiting", "it is waiting on you: " + wwhy))
        settled = True
    elif who == "clock" and st not in TERMINAL + QUEUED:
        out.append(("held", None, "waiting on the clock, not on you: " + wwhy))   # never an `ask`: there is nothing to answer
        settled = True
    elif live and st in QUEUED + ("blocked", "waiting"):
        out.append(("fix", "running", "a worker is in its worktree" if f["live"]
                    else "a live session is doing it in a subagent"))
        settled = True
    elif not live and st in ("running", "waiting") and f["age"] >= f.get("grace", GRACE):
        settled = True                                  # nobody is working on it: the board's own word for that is decided here, once
        if jk == "DONE" and pr == "MERGED":
            out.append(("fix", "merged", "it finished and its PR merged"))
        elif st == "waiting" and (jk == "DONE" or pr == "OPEN"):
            # `waiting` IS A PERSON'S WORD, like a session's kind: somebody typed it because what is left — landing
            # this PR, answering this question — is theirs to do. Reconciling it to `review` erases that, and the
            # pending watchdog below, which leaves a `waiting` row alone, would nag it on the very next tick
            # (lessons#10, owner-gated merging, told five times; owner, 2026-09-01). Only the PR merging, above,
            # moves it — that is a fact, not a word. A finish with no PR at all is still worth saying, with no
            # status, so it is reported and the word stands.
            if not has_pr:
                out.append(("ask", None, "it finished but no PR was ever opened"))
            elif pr == "CLOSED":
                out.append(("ask", None, "its PR was closed without merging"))
        elif jk == "DONE" and has_pr:
            out.append(("fix", "review", "it finished and its PR is open"))
        elif jk == "DONE":
            out.append(("ask", "review", "it finished but no PR was ever opened"))
        elif f.get("loop_end"):
            out.append(("ask", "blocked", "its loop stopped — " + f["loop_end"]))
        elif pr == "MERGED":
            out.append(("fix", "merged", "its PR merged"))
        elif pr == "OPEN":
            out.append(("fix", "review", "its PR is open"))
        else:
            out.append(("ask", "blocked", f.get("agent_stale")
                        or "no worker, and nothing says why (killed, or the box restarted)"))
    if not settled:                                     # the PR is the harder fact: it outranks any status but a live process
        if pr == "MERGED" and st != "merged":
            out.append(("fix", "merged", "its PR merged"))
        elif pr == "OPEN" and st in TERMINAL:
            out.append(("fix", "review", "its PR is still open"))
        elif pr == "CLOSED" and st not in TERMINAL:
            out.append(("ask", None, "its PR was closed without merging"))
        elif pr == "?":
            out.append(("info", None, "could not read its PR state"))
    if st == "review" and not has_pr and f.get("has_remote"):
        out.append(("ask", None, "it is waiting on a PR that was never opened"))
    # THE THREE RULES BELOW ARE ABOUT A ROW STILL BEING WORKED ON, so they read the word THIS RUN landed on rather
    # than the one the board still carries: on the very tick a PR merges, the block above has already said `merged`
    # while `st` says `review`. No record with a status and the board's own word stands — effective()'s rule.
    word = next((s for _, s, _ in out if s), "") or st
    finished = word in TERMINAL     # `merged`/`done`: the work landed, and what is left in the worktree is a leftover
    # …AND A `review` ROW WHOSE PR IS ON GITHUB NEVER HAD A WORKTREE HERE. Work reaches a row two ways: a track
    # worker in ~/.cc/worktrees/<repo>/<track>, and a planning session that did the work in a worktree of its own
    # and opened a PR from it. The second leaves the row a PR number and no track worktree, which is not a fault —
    # six such rows drew this ask in one digest and would have on every tick until they merged (owner, 2026-09-04).
    # IT READS THE BOARD'S OWN WORD, not the one this run landed on, unlike the rules around it: a `running` row
    # this run has just moved to `review` still CLAIMED a worker was in a worktree, and is asked. So is a `review`
    # row whose PR state is CLOSED or could not be read — only OPEN or MERGED is GitHub saying it has the branch.
    # WHAT THE SILENCE COSTS: commits a deleted worktree never pushed are not seen here at all — not by this rule
    # and not by the branch rule below, which reads `.cc/push.err` and `unpushed` from INSIDE the worktree
    # (facts()) and so goes quiet with it. That is acceptable for these rows and no others: the open or merged PR
    # is the evidence the branch reached origin, and `cc rm` refuses a worktree that still holds uncommitted work.
    landed_elsewhere = st == "review" and has_pr and pr in ("OPEN", "MERGED")
    # WORD IS A PROXY FOR "WAS THIS EVER DISPATCHED", AND THE PROXY BREAKS: `cc-board status`/`note` write ANY
    # word straight onto a row, dispatch or no. `lessons/tutor-on-a-member-lesson` was `queued`, never claimed —
    # only a task.md, no worktree ever — and a session, seeing it could not proceed yet, set it `blocked` with a
    # note explaining why. `word` left QUEUED on that one note and this rule read the empty worktree as loss
    # (raised 2026-09-11). `delivery` is a fact, not the guess: cc-task writes delivery.json the moment a row is
    # actually claimed, in STATE rather than the worktree, so it is still there to read after the worktree is
    # gone. But `delivery` alone reads two dispatch paths (worker, subagent) and misses a third: `cc <repo>
    # <track>`, run by hand (interactive, `__run`/`__runmember`), calls ensure_track and sets the row `running`
    # without ever calling `cc-task claim` — `lessons/pdf-companions` today has a worktree, `blocked`, and no
    # delivery.json. That path is the board's own `started` flag, set true by `__run`/`__runmember` and by
    # cc-handoff when a session opens the track, so a row it dispatched is caught even with no claim on file. A
    # row `word` moved off QUEUED by hand, with no claim and never started, is exempted exactly as a `queued` one
    # is; a row any dispatch path actually touched keeps being asked about, whatever its word says now.
    if (not f["wt_exists"] and not finished and word not in QUEUED
            and (f.get("delivery") or f.get("started") or f.get("worked"))
            and not f.get("agent") and not landed_elsewhere):
        out.append(("ask", None, "its worktree is gone"))   # a queued track has never been started: it is SUPPOSED to have no worktree
    # …AND NEITHER HAS A ROW A SESSION IS WORKING IN ITS SUBAGENTS: nothing dispatches such a row, so `ensure_track`
    # never made it a worktree. The mark flips a `queued` one to `running` above, so `word` leaves QUEUED and this
    # rule fired on it — every marked row traded one NEEDS YOU line for another, printed and paged on each
    # 20-minute run, which is the opposite of what the mark is for (review of #242).
    # IT IS THE MARK THAT SILENCES THIS, NOT A LIVE ONE. A cold mark is still a row nothing ever dispatched, and the
    # `running` word the mark gave it while it was warm stays on the board after it goes cold: guarding on
    # `agent_live` meant a row marked while `queued` was reported "marked … 6 h ago, mark expired" AND "its worktree
    # is gone" on the same run — a second NEEDS YOU line this rule invented for a row that had none before the mark
    # existed (review of #242, round 2). The cold half is already said once, by the grace rule above.
    # …AND A FINISHED ROW'S BRANCH IS SUPPOSED TO BE GONE. cc-land deletes it on merge, so `origin/<branch>` is
    # absent, branch_unpushed() can only say "cannot tell", and a `.cc/push.err` nothing ever clears turned that
    # into "<repo>/land-queue-retry — its branch is not pushed" about PR #154, which HAD merged: the branch is gone
    # because the merge deleted it, which is success, not a fault (owner, 2026-09-02). 88 of this repo's 98 rows are
    # `merged`, so it fires on any of them a pass reaches. The worktree rule above always said this; these two did
    # not. Nothing is hidden by the silence: work a merged worktree still holds uncommitted is cc-janitor rule 1's
    # to find, and rule 1 REPORTS such a worktree rather than removing it.
    # THE TWO ASKS BELOW ARE THEIR OWN CLASS, kept apart because the hold rule at the end of this function has to
    # tell them from the rest: every other ask is the box guessing at a NEXT MOVE, these two are the box saying
    # work is about to be LOST. See the hold rule for what that costs if they are muted.
    safety = []
    if f.get("commit_err") and not finished:
        safety.append(("ask", None, "its last checkpoint would not commit — that work is only in the worktree"))
    if f.get("push_err") and (f.get("unpushed") is True or (f.get("unpushed") is None and not finished)):
        # THE MARKER IS NOT THE ANSWER — see branch_unpushed(). True is "origin really does not have it", None is
        # "could not tell", and a plain "origin has it" always silences the finding.
        # ONLY THE "COULD NOT TELL" HALF IS FORGIVEN A FINISHED ROW, and the halves are NOT interchangeable here.
        # None is what the incident produced: the merge deleted the branch, so `origin/<branch>` does not resolve
        # and there is nothing to compare against. True needs that ref to RESOLVE and HEAD to be unreachable from
        # it — commits origin really lacks — which a merge cannot cause. Silencing True on a merged row would hide
        # the one case that loses work: committed-but-unpushed is not `dirty`, so cc-janitor rule 1 reads it as
        # clean, returns `rm`, and `cc rm` runs `git branch -D track/<x>` 48h later with those commits in it. The
        # comment above is about UNCOMMITTED work, which rule 1 does report; this is the other half (review #203).
        safety.append(("ask", None, "its branch is not pushed"))
    out += safety
    # A ROW SOMEBODY IS DELIBERATELY SITTING ON RAISES NOTHING A PERSON HAS TO ANSWER (cc-board's `held`). Every
    # rule above judges a row by its status and the box around it, and neither can say "a person stopped this,
    # and here is what has to happen first". So this tool nagged four times in one day about rows that were held
    # on purpose — and one of those was #324, whose approval had been WITHDRAWN because landing it before its
    # ordering was a signed-in visitor running code as the owner. A watcher that reads only status was telling a
    # reader, in the box's own voice and on a schedule, to go and do the thing three parties had blocked.
    # WHAT A HOLD MUTES IS THE NAG CLASS AND NOTHING ELSE — "it looks abandoned", "its worktree is gone", "its PR
    # was closed", and the "review and land it" offer in the pending watchdog. Those are the box reading a status
    # and guessing at a next move, which is the guess a hold answers. THREE THINGS SURVIVE IT:
    #   · the silent `fix`es, so the board keeps telling the truth about a merged PR or a live worker;
    #   · a `stop` — a hold is a person's word about what to do next, never a licence to burn tokens;
    #   · `safety` and the PERSON-ask, which are the HOST's, not a guess about a next move. cc-janitor's 48 h
    #     branch delete keeps running under a hold, so muting "its branch is not pushed" would feed a held row's
    #     only copy of some commits to `git branch -D` with nobody told; and the person-ask is what puts the row
    #     on the `waiting` word — the owner's NEEDS YOU list — so a held row's question still reaches him.
    # THAT IS ALSO WHY THE LINE IS DRAWN HERE and not around "ask". On a member workspace board `held` is
    # member-writable: a member can quiet the guesses about their own row, which is the point of the word, but
    # cannot quiet the host telling them work is about to be lost, or a question they were asked.
    # The row says `held` beside all of it, which prints and is counted, because a hold nobody can see is the
    # board note this replaces. (`held` is the same word the clock rule above uses for the same reason: no ask,
    # nothing to answer.) The pending watchdog reads f["held"] itself — it never calls decide().
    hold = str(f.get("held") or "").strip()   # stripped HERE too: a word that is only spaces holds nothing, and
    if hold:                                  # this rule must not depend on which caller built the facts
        out = [r for r in out if r[0] in ("fix", "stop") or r in safety
               or (r[0], r[1]) == ("ask", "waiting")] + [("held", None, "held: " + hold)]
    return out


def effective(f, recs):
    """Pure — THE ONE WORD for a track (review rec #7: this tool is the sole decider). What decide() would set
    outranks the board, because decide() has just looked at the box and the board is a file somebody wrote between
    turns. No record with a status means nothing here disagrees, and the board's own word stands."""
    session = (f.get("kind") or "track") == "session"   # a channel's word is its kind (cc-board `list` says the same):
    word = next((st for _, st, _ in recs if st), "") or (f["status"] or "queued")
    if session:                                          # cc ls and cc digest never print `running` for one — `waiting`,
        return "session" if word != "waiting" else word  # the owner's question, is the one track word it wears
    # …and `idle` is the word for the other half of that: RUNNING is a claim that something is happening, and a session
    # parked at its prompt makes no such claim. Only ever narrows `running` — every word decide() reached first
    # (`waiting`, `blocked`, `merged`) is a fact about the work, and outranks a fact about the keyboard.
    if word == "todo":
        return "queued"     # one vocabulary (see QUEUED): the consumers print `queued`, and never a second word for it
    return "idle" if word == "running" and idle_session(f) else word


def pending(f, state):
    """Pure — finished, PR open, nobody landed it. Nothing is WRONG here, which is the point: decide() has no
    record to make and the owner sees a consistent board. `?` and None are not OPEN — a PR state that could not
    be read, or was never asked for, is not evidence that work is sitting. A board that says `waiting` is never
    pending: a PERSON typed that word, and where merging is theirs (lessons#10) landing IS what they are being
    waited on for. Read from the board, not from `state`, so the rule holds however the row is reconciled — and
    decide() leaves a `waiting` word alone, so it holds on every tick, not just the first (review of #150)."""
    return state == "review" and (f.get("pr_state") or "") == "OPEN" and f["status"] != "waiting"


LAND_ARGV = re.compile(r"(?:^|[\s/])cc-land\s+(?:queue\s+)?([A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9._-]*)"
                       r"((?:\s+(?:--no-review|--re-review|[0-9]+))+)(?=\s|$)")


def pr_number(url):
    """The number a board row's `pr` ends in (a URL, `#12`, or just the digits); None when there is none."""
    m = re.search(r"([0-9]+)/?\s*$", str(url or ""))
    return int(m.group(1)) if m else None


def landing():
    """{(repo, pr)} somebody is already landing: named by a live cc-land argv (the PR path, or `queue`), by a job
    under ~/.cc/state/land/ — cc-land keeps that file until the landing is said and done — or by a fresh work
    record under ~/.cc/state/land/root/, which is the one thing that spans the GAPS between a landing's rounds.
    Nagging a session about one of these is telling it to do what is being done (nine lines a tick, 2026-09-01)."""
    out = set()
    for path in glob.glob(f"{HOME}/.cc/state/land/*.json"):
        try:
            j = json.load(open(path))
            if j.get("repo") and j.get("pr"):
                out.add((j["repo"], int(j["pr"])))
        except Exception:
            continue                                # half-written, or a catch-up job with no PR: no PR named
    # BETWEEN ROUNDS IS STILL BEING LANDED. The job file is a round, not the landing: a LAND-AFTER-FIX repair, a
    # spent review budget the seat must decide, a conflict merge — each ends the round and deletes the file minutes
    # before the next one is queued. cc-land's work record outlives the round that wrote it and goes when the PR
    # lands, so a record touched within BETWEEN_ROUNDS says a landing is mid-flight; an older one is only history.
    for path in glob.glob(f"{HOME}/.cc/state/land/root/*.json"):
        try:
            if now() - os.path.getmtime(path) > BETWEEN_ROUNDS:
                continue
            j = json.load(open(path))
            if j.get("repo") and j.get("pr"):
                out.add((j["repo"], int(j["pr"])))
        except Exception:
            continue
    me = os.getpid()
    for d in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not d.isdigit() or int(d) == me:
            continue
        try:
            argv = open(f"/proc/{d}/cmdline", "rb").read().decode(errors="replace").split("\0")
        except OSError:
            continue
        if any(os.path.basename(a) == "claude" for a in argv):
            continue                                # a session's prompt QUOTES the command; it is not running it
        for m in LAND_ARGV.finditer(" ".join(argv)):
            out.update((m.group(1), int(n)) for n in re.findall(r"[0-9]+", m.group(2)))
    return out


def snapshot(rows, limit=0.0):
    """The read-only answer to "what is every track, really", as a dict ready for ~/.cc/state/reconcile.json.

    ONE DECIDER, NOT A SECOND TRUTH. The board holds the word; this holds the three things one word cannot — who a
    track is waiting on (a person, the clock, or nobody), why, and whether anything is alive — so `cc-digest` and
    `cc ls`, which have no process table of their own, print what the Home tab prints instead of each guessing.
    It is an OUTPUT: nothing here ever reads it back, and a consumer that finds it missing or stale falls back to
    the board rather than going blind."""
    return {"at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime()), "epoch": int(now()),
            "limit_until": int(limit or 0),
            "tracks": {f"{repo}/{track}": {"state": state, "board": f["status"],
                                           "kind": f.get("kind") or "track",   # `session`: a channel's row, never a task
                                           # a subagent is alive too — the mark's window is what says so
                                           "live": bool(f["live"] or f.get("agent_live")),
                                           "waiting_on": who, "why": why,
                                           # seconds since the track's pane last moved: what tells `idle` from `running`
                                           "idle_for": int(f.get("pane_idle") or 0),
                                           # …and the three facts the Home tab and cc-pulse used to read for themselves:
                                           # a live `cc-loop` (the only thing that makes an iteration count current),
                                           # a live session's `agent` mark (a subagent has the row), a person's hold
                                           "loop": bool(f.get("loops")), "agent": bool(f.get("agent_live")),
                                           "held": str(f.get("held") or "").strip()}
                       for repo, track, f, state, who, why in rows}}


# ── the run ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def paused(target):
    """`cc-pause on <repo>` parks a whole project — its repo, its tracks and its orchs — and nothing the box does
    on its own may spend on it until `cc-pause off`. cc-pause owns that state; asking it is the only reading of it
    there is. It cannot run (no such sibling on this box, a timeout): behave exactly as this file did before the
    pause existed — a pause must never be the reason a pass stops working."""
    try:
        return subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-pause", "is", str(target)], stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                              capture_output=True, timeout=10).returncode == 0
    except Exception:
        return False


def boards():
    """Every REAL sidecar board, sorted: (path, board or None if unreadable). A suite that died mid-run left a
    fixture board whose `waiting` row was reconciled, snapshotted and shown to the owner as real (2026-09-01):
    a `_cc<suite><pid>` board is a board only to a process under <pid>, and cc-board's `reals` says which these
    are — one batch, never a copy of its rule; cc-janitor --fixtures sweeps the rest once their pid is gone."""
    out = []
    for n in ccboard().reals(sorted(os.path.basename(fn) for fn in glob.glob(f"{BOARDS}/*.json"))):
        fn = f"{BOARDS}/{n}"
        try:
            out.append((fn, board_read(fn)))
        except Exception:
            out.append((fn, None))
    return out


def ccboard():
    """cc-board imported as a module (the way cc-time is), beside THIS copy, not a stubbed BIN: its opener `read`, and its
    `reals` — the one rule for which of ~/.cc/boards is a board and which is a suite's fixture. This file carried the
    rule itself until 2026-09-11 and two others read `_cctest` alone; now every reader asks (cc-board FIXTURE)."""
    if not hasattr(ccboard, "mod"):
        import importlib.machinery, importlib.util
        s = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("ccboard", importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(
            "ccboard", os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "cc-board")))
        ccboard.mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(s); s.loader.exec_module(ccboard.mod)
    return ccboard.mod


def board_read(path):
    """A board file, read through cc-board's opener: a member's board is a link into a directory the MEMBER writes, and
    a plain open here followed whatever it planted — a pipe hung this tool for good (review of #224). None = no board
    there; cc-board's Refused for anything planted, which a caller treats as unreadable."""
    return ccboard().read(path)


def facts(repos, net=True, grace=GRACE, no_stop=False, wedged=WEDGED, mark=AGENT_MARK):
    """Every board track, with what the box says about it. Yields (repo, track, board_track, facts)."""
    live, ppids, sessions, workers, loops = procs()
    tlive, pane_idle, wins = tmux_live(ppids, sessions)
    live |= tlive
    seen, limited = ledger(f"{STATE}/wedged.json"), limit_active()
    started = limit_started()   # read once: every row's mark is weighed against the same episode
    prs, remotes, t0 = 0, {}, now()
    for fn, d in boards():
        if d is None:
            continue                                    # one unreadable board must not hide every other repo
        repo = d.get("repo") or os.path.basename(fn)[:-5]
        if repos and repo not in repos:
            continue
        for track, t in sorted(d.get("tracks", {}).items()):
            st = t.get("status", "")
            pr = t.get("pr", "")
            want_pr = bool(pr) and st != "merged"   # `merged` is the one status this tool derives FROM the PR: re-reading it every tick buys nothing
            ask_pr = want_pr and prs < PRMAX        # over the cap the PR is UNKNOWN, not absent — a run that ran out of budget says so
            prs += bool(ask_pr)
            wt = t.get("worktree") or f"{WTS}/{repo}/{track}"
            if repo not in remotes:
                remotes[repo] = has_remote(repo)
            key = f"{repo}/{track}"
            # cc-board's mark: the window of the session doing this row in a subagent. A SESSION SPEAKS FOR ITS
            # OWN REPO AND NO OTHER — `cc` names a planning window for its repo, and without that test any window
            # on the box could hold any repo's row open, which is exactly the reach a shared board must not give.
            ag = (t.get("agent") or "").strip()
            ag_live, ag_stale = mark_why(t, wins, repo, t0, mark, started)
            f = {
                "agent": ag, "agent_live": ag_live, "agent_stale": ag_stale,
                "held": str(t.get("held") or "").strip(),   # cc-board's `held`: a person stopped this, and why
                "repo": repo, "track": track, "status": st, "pr": pr, "kind": t.get("kind") or "track",   # decide() narrows by kind
                "pr_state": pr_state(repo, pr, net) if ask_pr else ("?" if want_pr and net else None),
                "live": f"{repo}/{track}" in live, "wt_exists": os.path.isdir(wt),
                "delivery": os.path.exists(f"{STATE}/{repo}/{track}/delivery.json"),   # cc-task's claim record:
                # written the moment a row is actually claimed (worker or subagent), lives in STATE so it
                # outlives the worktree it was claimed for — see the worktree rule below for why this exists.
                "started": bool(t.get("started")),   # the board's own flag: set true by `cc <repo> <track>`
                # (interactive, __run/__runmember) and by cc-handoff when a session opens the track — the
                # dispatch path `delivery` above misses, since none of them call `cc-task claim`.
                "worked": os.path.exists(f"{STATE}/{repo}/{track}/progress.md")
                          or os.path.exists(f"{STATE}/{repo}/{track}/loop.log"),   # the legacy worker path's own
                # trace: `cc <repo> <track> --go` predates cc-task claim and sets neither `delivery` nor
                # `started`, but a row it actually ran left a journal or a loop log — a never-dispatched row has
                # only task.md (per the brief).
                "age": max(0.0, t0 - epoch(t.get("updated") or t.get("created"))), "grace": grace,
                "journal": journal(repo, track), "loop_end": loop_end(repo, track),
                "commit_err": os.path.exists(commit_marker(repo, track)),
                "push_err": os.path.exists(push_marker(repo, track)), "has_remote": remotes[repo],
                "unpushed": branch_unpushed(wt) if os.path.exists(push_marker(repo, track)) else False,
                "workers": workers.get(key, []), "loops": loops.get(key, []), "limited": limited,
                "orphans": workers.get(key, []) if not loops.get(key) else [],
                "idle": idle_for(repo, track, wt) if workers.get(key) else 0.0,
                "pane_idle": pane_idle.get(key, 0.0)}   # …and this one is the KEYBOARD's: see idle_session()
            f["stop_why"] = "" if no_stop else runaway(f, t0 - float(seen.get(key) or 0) <= WEDGE_SEEN, wedged)
            f["wedged_now"] = bool(f["workers"] and not f["limited"] and f["idle"] >= wedged)
            yield repo, track, t, f


def orphans(repos, net=True, grace=GRACE, busy=None):
    """Worktrees with no board entry at all — real work the board has never heard of. Reported only: inventing
    a track from a directory name is the kind of guess this tool exists to avoid.

    A DELIVERY IN FLIGHT IS NOT ONE. A planning session's builder works a row in a worktree named for its BRANCH,
    not for the row, so no board entry will ever carry that name however well the row is kept — and this went out
    twice on 2026-09-11 about worktrees whose PRs were open (one of them queued in the landing), each costing the
    seat a wake-up and a read that found nothing to do. Four things say delivery rather than leftover, and the
    worktree is skipped on any of them: a row NAMES this directory (whatever the row is called), it was made inside
    `grace` (the same window a new track gets to appear in), its branch has an OPEN PR, or its branch's PR is one
    somebody is landing — landing(), the same reader #429 gave the PR nag: a job file, a live cc-land argv, or a
    work record between two rounds. The queue LOG is not consulted: it keeps every landing this box ever ran, so a
    PR merged last week would read as in flight forever.

    …AND A FIFTH: A MERGE THAT HAS JUST HAPPENED, which is the one the four above still missed. The merge, the
    worktree's removal and this pass race each other, and between them there are minutes in which the PR is no
    longer OPEN, the landing has taken its job file and its work record with it, and the directory is still there.
    A person was paged about exactly that on 2026-09-11: PR #434 merged at 16:29:43Z and the 16:31:29Z pass called
    its worktree one the board had never heard of, 106 seconds later. So a MERGED PR forgives the worktree for
    BETWEEN_ROUNDS — the same window a landing's own gaps get — and no longer: a worktree still sitting there an
    hour after its PR landed is a leftover somebody should clear, and saying so is this rule's whole job. Nothing
    else is forgiven — with no network the branch cannot be looked up and the worktree is reported exactly as it
    always was."""
    known, claimed = set(), set()
    for fn, d in boards():
        if d is None:
            return []                                   # an unreadable board makes every track look orphaned: say nothing
        repo = d.get("repo") or os.path.basename(fn)[:-5]
        for t, tr in (d.get("tracks") or {}).items():
            known.add(f"{repo}/{t}")
            wt = (tr or {}).get("worktree")              # a row may name a directory of its own: that is a board entry too
            if wt:
                claimed.add(os.path.realpath(os.path.expanduser(wt)))
    out, asked, t0 = [], 0, now()
    for p in sorted(glob.glob(f"{WTS}/*/*")):
        repo, track = p.split(os.sep)[-2:]
        if not ccboard().real(repo):                    # a suite's `_cc<suite><pid>` scaffolding (a leaked `_ccsbx<pid>` was
            continue                                    # reported as two worktrees of work, 2026-09-02), not work: `known` never has it either
        if not os.path.isdir(p) or f"{repo}/{track}" in known or (repos and repo not in repos):
            continue
        if os.path.realpath(p) in claimed:
            continue
        try:
            if now() - os.path.getmtime(p) < grace:     # `git worktree add` stamps the directory: minutes old is a
                continue                                # delivery still being set up, not one the board never heard of
        except OSError:
            pass
        prs = None
        if asked < ORPHANPRS:
            asked += 1                                  # counted whether or not GitHub answered: the cap is on asking
            prs = branch_prs(repo, wt_branch(p), net)
        if prs:
            busy = landing() if busy is None else busy  # one process-table read a run, and only with a PR to match
            if any(st == "OPEN" or (repo, n) in busy or (st == "MERGED" and t0 - at <= BETWEEN_ROUNDS)
                   for n, st, at in prs):
                continue
        out.append((repo, track, ("ask", None, "a worktree with no board entry")))
    return out


def end_it(pids):
    """SIGTERM to each process's whole group — the worker and the tools it started, exactly the care `cc … --go`
    takes with an orphan. Never our own group, and a process that is already gone counts as ended."""
    mine, ok = os.getpgid(0), False
    for pid in pids:
        try:
            pg = os.getpgid(pid)
        except OSError:
            ok = True                                   # it left between the walk and here: that is the outcome we wanted
            continue
        try:
            if pg != mine:
                os.killpg(pg, signal.SIGTERM)
            else:
                os.kill(pid, signal.SIGTERM)            # not a group leader of its own: take just it
            ok = True
        except OSError:
            pass
    return ok


INVESTIGATE_KEY = "CC_FAILURES_INVESTIGATE"   # the one named setting (decision 5): `cc-config set CC_FAILURES_INVESTIGATE on`


def investigate():
    """Claim one eligible failure cluster and launch its bounded investigation (docstring: THE FAILURE INVESTIGATION).
    Returns the line to print, or None when there was nothing to say. Never raises: a timer pass is not to die on this."""
    def run(*a, timeout=30):
        try:
            return subprocess.run(a, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)
        except Exception:
            return None
    p = run(f"{BIN}/cc-config", "get", INVESTIGATE_KEY)
    if not p or p.stdout.strip().lower() != "on":
        return None                                 # inert until the owner turns it on; no line, no claim
    p = run(f"{BIN}/cc-failures", "eligible")
    if not p or p.returncode != 0 or not p.stdout.strip():
        return None                                 # nothing eligible: a quiet day adds nothing
    # "the launch checks `cc-tier allows explore` and stands down when it says no, the cluster staying eligible and
    # visible with its age" — no claim is taken, so `cc board failures` keeps saying `unclaimed <age>`
    t = run(f"{BIN}/cc-tier", "allows", "explore")
    if not t or t.returncode != 0:
        n = sum(1 for ln in p.stdout.splitlines() if ln.strip() and "\tunclaimed" in ln)
        return f"  held       failure investigation: the tier holds explore work; {n} eligible cluster(s) wait, unclaimed" if n else None
    launcher = os.environ.get("CC_RECONCILE_LAUNCH")   # tests: a stub in place of the unit
    me = os.getpid()                                # one at a time: an investigation still running holds the next
    # The scan is box-wide, so a test must not run it: with the setting on, the box's own investigation in flight
    # (two passes, up to 20 min) would hold a fixture's launch and turn the selfcheck red (the same process-table
    # flake selftest.sh records fixing). A stubbed launcher runs nothing that outlives the pass, so nothing to hold on.
    for d in [] if launcher else os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not d.isdigit() or int(d) == me:
            continue
        try:
            argv = open(f"/proc/{d}/cmdline", "rb").read().decode(errors="replace").split("\0")
        except OSError:
            continue
        if any(os.path.basename(a) == "claude" for a in argv):
            continue                                # a session's prompt QUOTES the command; it is not running it
        if any(os.path.basename(a) == "cc-audit" and argv[i + 1:i + 2] == ["failure"] for i, a in enumerate(argv)):
            return None
    c = run(f"{BIN}/cc-failures", "claim")
    if not c or c.returncode != 0:
        return None                                 # claimed already, or the day's slots spent: cc-failures said why
    claim, cluster, scope, kind = (c.stdout.rstrip("\n").split("\t") + ["", "", "", ""])[:4]
    if launcher:
        r = run(launcher, "failure", claim); how = "stub" if r and r.returncode == 0 else "stub failed"
    else:
        # A transient unit, not a child: this pass is a oneshot service, and a child in its control group dies with it
        # minutes before the model answers — the same reason the lander's queue worker is started this way.
        unit = f"cc-audit-failure-{claim}"
        r = run("systemd-run", "--user", "--collect", "--quiet", f"--unit={unit}",
                "--description=cc-audit: one bounded failure investigation", f"{BIN}/cc-audit", "failure", claim, timeout=120)
        if r and r.returncode == 0:
            how = unit
        else:
            try:
                subprocess.Popen([f"{BIN}/cc-audit", "failure", claim], start_new_session=True,
                                 stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
                how = "a detached child (no systemd --user here)"
            except Exception as e:
                how = f"launch failed: {e.__class__.__name__}"
    try:
        os.makedirs(STATE, exist_ok=True)
        with open(f"{STATE}/reconcile.log", "a") as log:
            log.write(f"{time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ', time.gmtime())}\tinvestigation\t{claim} {cluster} {scope}/{kind}\t{how}\n")
    except OSError:
        pass
    return f"  investigation  {claim} {scope}/{kind} ({cluster}) — cc-audit failure launched: {how}"


def board(*args):
    try:
        return subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-board", *args], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30).returncode == 0
    except Exception:
        return False


def ledger(path, write=None):
    """The 'already told the owner' ledger: {finding: when}. A finding that persists is worth one push a day,
    not one every timer tick — and one that has gone away is dropped, so its return is news again."""
    if write is None:
        try:
            return json.load(open(path))
        except Exception:
            return {}
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
    tmp = path + ".tmp"
    json.dump(write, open(tmp, "w"))
    os.replace(tmp, path)
    return write


def view():
    """The snapshot as its shell readers take it — `cc ls` and cc-digest — so the freshness rule and the row shape live
    here once: one TSV row per track, repo/track · state · waiting_on · why, escaped as jq's @tsv would, so a `read -r`
    over it needs no thought. Missing, unreadable, not JSON, or past FRESH: no rows and exit 0 — the reader keeps the
    board's own word, and never blocks on this."""
    try:
        snap = json.load(open(f"{STATE}/reconcile.json"))
        epoch = snap.get("epoch") if isinstance(snap, dict) else None
        if not isinstance(epoch, int) or epoch <= 0 or now() - epoch >= FRESH:
            return 0
        tracks = snap.get("tracks")
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return 0

    def cell(v):   # jq: `.x // ""` blanks null and false; @tsv escapes the four characters a TSV cannot carry
        s = "" if v is None or v is False else str(v)
        return s.replace("\\", "\\\\").replace("\t", "\\t").replace("\n", "\\n").replace("\r", "\\r")
    rows = ["\t".join(cell(x) for x in (k, t.get("state"), t.get("waiting_on"), t.get("why")))
            for k, t in (tracks.items() if isinstance(tracks, dict) else ()) if isinstance(t, dict)]
    sys.stdout.buffer.write("".join(r + "\n" for r in rows).encode("utf-8"))   # the bytes jq gave, whatever the caller's locale
    return 0


def main(argv):
    if argv[:1] == ["selfcheck"]:
        return selfcheck()
    if argv[:1] == ["view"]:
        return view()
    snap_only = argv[:1] == ["snapshot"]
    if snap_only:
        argv = argv[1:] + ["--no-net"]              # a snapshot is a local read: it must never stall on GitHub
        if any(a in ("--apply", "--notify") for a in argv):
            print("cc-reconcile: snapshot writes nothing — it takes no --apply or --notify", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    repos, apply_, notify, as_json, quiet, net, grace, repeat = [], False, False, False, False, True, GRACE, REPEAT
    no_stop, wedged = False, int(os.environ.get("CC_RECONCILE_WEDGED") or WEDGED)   # env: tests do not wait three hours
    mark = float(os.environ.get("CC_AGENT_MARK_HOURS") or AGENT_MARK / 3600) * 3600
    pend_after = int(os.environ.get("CC_PENDING_ALERT") or PENDING)
    while argv:
        a = argv.pop(0)
        if a == "--apply":
            apply_ = True
        elif a == "--no-stop":
            no_stop = True
        elif a == "--notify":
            notify = True
        elif a == "--json":
            as_json = True
        elif a in ("-q", "--quiet"):
            quiet = True
        elif a == "--no-net":
            net = False
        elif a in ("--grace", "--repeat"):
            if not argv or not argv[0].lstrip("-").isdigit():
                print(f"cc-reconcile: {a} needs a number of seconds", file=sys.stderr)
                return 2
            v = int(argv.pop(0))
            grace, repeat = (v, repeat) if a == "--grace" else (grace, v)
        elif a in ("-h", "--help"):
            print(__doc__.strip())
            return 0
        elif a.startswith("-"):
            print(f"cc-reconcile: unknown option {a}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        else:
            repos.append(a)
    for r in repos:   # a named repo with no board is a typo, not "nothing to reconcile"
        if not os.path.exists(f"{BOARDS}/{r}.json"):
            print(f"cc-reconcile: no board for '{r}'", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    recs, tracks, wedging, snaprows = [], 0, {}, []
    for repo, track, t, f in facts(repos, net, grace, no_stop, wedged, mark):
        tracks += 1
        if f.get("wedged_now"):
            wedging[f"{repo}/{track}"] = now()          # one strike; the next run 20 min from now decides
        mine = decide(f)
        state = effective(f, mine)
        who, why = waits_for(f) if state not in TERMINAL else ("", "")   # a finished track waits on nobody, whatever
        snaprows.append((repo, track, f, state, who, why))               # its journal still says three weeks later
        for kind, st, why_ in mine:
            rec = {"repo": repo, "track": track, "kind": kind, "from": f["status"], "to": st, "why": why_}
            if kind == "stop":
                rec["pids"] = f["workers"] + f["loops"]
            recs.append(rec)
    if not snap_only:                                   # `snapshot` is pure: the wedged strike count is the timer's to keep
        ledger(f"{STATE}/wedged.json", wedging)         # only what is still wedged survives: one that recovers starts over
    for repo, track, (kind, st, why) in orphans(repos, net, grace):
        recs.append({"repo": repo, "track": track, "kind": kind, "from": "", "to": st, "why": why})
    for r in recs:
        if r["kind"] == "stop":         # the one record that ENDS something instead of describing it
            r["applied"] = bool(apply_ and end_it(r["pids"])
                                and board("status", r["repo"], r["track"], "blocked")
                                and board("note", r["repo"], r["track"], f"stopped by cc-reconcile: {r['why']}"))
            continue
        r["applied"] = bool(apply_ and r["to"] and r["to"] != r["from"]
                            and board("status", r["repo"], r["track"], r["to"])
                            and board("note", r["repo"], r["track"],
                                      f"reconciled: {r['from']} → {r['to']} ({r['why']})"))
    if apply_ and any(r["applied"] for r in recs):
        os.makedirs(STATE, exist_ok=True)
        with open(f"{STATE}/reconcile.log", "a") as log:
            for r in (x for x in recs if x["applied"]):
                what = "STOPPED" if r["kind"] == "stop" else f"{r['from']} -> {r['to']}"
                log.write(f"{time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ', time.gmtime())}\t"
                          f"{r['repo']}/{r['track']}\t{what}\t{r['why']}\n")
    snap = snapshot(snaprows, limit_until())
    if snap_only:                                   # pure: decided and printed, and nothing on disk is different for it
        print(json.dumps(snap, indent=2))
        return 0
    if not repos:                                   # a filtered run knows about one repo: it must never clobber the whole box's
        try:
            os.makedirs(STATE, exist_ok=True)
            tmp = f"{STATE}/reconcile.json.tmp"
            json.dump(snap, open(tmp, "w"), indent=2)
            os.replace(tmp, f"{STATE}/reconcile.json")
        except OSError:
            pass                                    # a snapshot nobody could write is a consumer falling back, not a failed run
        try:                                        # same charter, different file: trust entries whose dir is gone are drift
            subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-trust", "prune"], capture_output=True, timeout=30)
        except Exception:
            pass
        try:                                        # and again: a handoff record is driven by a session's own Stop
            subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-handoff", "--sweep"], capture_output=True, timeout=60)
        except Exception:                           # hook, which a session that CRASHED no longer has. cc-handoff
            pass                                    # owns what it sweeps and how — this timer only gives it a turn
        try:                                        # and a test run's `_cc<suite><pid>` leftovers once that pid is gone:
            subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-janitor", "--fixtures"], capture_output=True, timeout=60)
        except Exception:                           # a board with a `waiting` row reached the owner as real (2026-09-01).
            pass                                    # cc-janitor owns the sweep (its daily run does it too); this is a turn
        if net:
            try:                                    # and #approvals: a card whose PR is in reads as pending until it is
                subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-slack", "approvals-sweep"], capture_output=True, timeout=180)
            except Exception:                       # edited (a merge in GitHub's UI, a card from before the edit
                pass                                # existed). cc-slack owns the card; this only gives it a turn
        if apply_:                                  # and one failure investigation, if the owner's setting and the tier allow (investigate())
            inv = investigate()
            if inv and not quiet and not as_json:
                print(inv)
    asks = [r for r in recs if r["kind"] in ("ask", "stop")]   # a runaway that was ended is still a person's to settle
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps({"tracks": tracks, "records": recs}, indent=2))
    elif not quiet:
        for r in recs:
            move = f"{r['from']} → {r['to']}" if r["to"] and r["to"] != r["from"] else ""
            word = {"ask": "needs you", "info": "unknown", "held": "held",
                    "stop": "stopped" if r["applied"] else "runaway"}.get(
                        r["kind"], "fixed" if r["applied"] else "would fix")
            print(f"  {word:<9}  {r['repo']}/{r['track']:<16} {move:<20} {r['why']}")
        info = sum(r["kind"] == "info" for r in recs)
        held = sum(r["kind"] == "held" for r in recs)   # known, and not the owner's: it says so in its own word
        print(" · ".join(x for x in (f"{len(recs) - len(asks) - info - held} {'fixed' if apply_ else 'to fix'}",
                                     f"{len(asks)} needs you", f"{held} held" if held else "",
                                     f"{info} unknown") if x) + f"  ({tracks} tracks)"
              if recs else f"the board matches reality ({tracks} tracks)")
    if notify:
        path = f"{STATE}/reconcile.seen.json"
        old, t0 = ledger(path), now()
        inj = os.environ.get("CC_RECONCILE_INJECT") or f"{BIN}/cc-slack"

        def offer(repo, line):
            """Hand a finding to that repo's live session. A dead daemon or no session window refuses."""
            try:
                return subprocess.run([inj, "inject", "--no-start", repo, line],
                                      capture_output=True, timeout=30).returncode == 0
            except Exception:
                return False

        # A PARKED PROJECT IS NOT NAGGED AND NOT PAGED — not its session, not the owner. `cc-pause on <repo>` stops
        # what the box does to a project on its own, and every wake and every page this file buys is below this
        # line; everything ABOVE it — the reconciling itself, the snapshot, `view` — still runs, so a parked
        # project's board stays as true as any other's. Its nag memos are not carried through the pause either:
        # every finding here is read off the board, which is still there, so each one is news again at the first
        # pass after the resume, with its "unlanded for Nh" clock counting from then instead of through the park.
        parked = {}

        def project_parked(repo):
            if repo not in parked:
                parked[repo] = paused(repo)
            return parked[repo]

        keys = {f"{r['repo']}/{r['track']}: {r['why']}": r
                for r in asks if not project_parked(r["repo"])}
        fresh = [r for k, r in keys.items() if t0 - float(old.get(k, 0) or 0) >= repeat]
        told = {f"{r['repo']}/{r['track']}: {r['why']}" for r in fresh}
        # PENDING — finished, PR open, unlanded. No `ask` above it and none coming: the board and the box agree.
        # Landing it is one command, not a decision — but the session hears it ONCE per PR, not every tick (nine
        # identical lines a tick for PRs already queued in land lanes; owner, 2026-09-01), and the owner only once
        # it has stood past the window, then once per window. Two rows are nobody's to nag: one the board says is
        # `waiting` (pending() itself says why), and one whose PR somebody is landing — a live cc-land argv, a job
        # under ~/.cc/state/land/, or a landing mid-flight between two rounds. Either drops the keys, so a PR that
        # falls out of a lane unlanded is news again, once.
        keep, busy = {}, None
        for repo, track, f, state, _, _ in snaprows:
            if project_parked(repo) or f.get("held"):
                # parked: no alert, no offer and no memo kept — the row itself is untouched on the board.
                # …AND A HELD ROW THE SAME WAY, for the same reason and with the same consequence: this is the
                # "review and land it" line, and it fired on a PR whose approval had been withdrawn (see
                # decide()). No memo is kept either, so the PR is news again, once, when the hold is lifted.
                continue
            k, ak = f"pending {repo}/{track}", f"alerted {repo}/{track}"
            ok_ = f"offered {repo}/{track} {f.get('pr') or ''}".rstrip()   # per PR: a new PR on the track is news again
            if not pending(f, state):
                # a poll that came back `?` (timeout, auth blip, rate limit) is no evidence either way: no nag
                # this run, but the clock keeps running. Only MERGED / CLOSED / None, or a board state that
                # left review, drops the keys.
                if state == "review" and f.get("pr_state") == "?":
                    keep.update({x: old[x] for x in (k, ak, ok_) if old.get(x)})
                continue
            busy = landing() if busy is None else busy   # one process-table read a run, and only with something pending
            if (repo, pr_number(f.get("pr"))) in busy:
                continue
            if refused(repo, f.get("pr"), net):
                # …AND A PR THE BOX HAS ALREADY REFUSED IS NOBODY'S TO NAG EITHER, on the same terms as `held`
                # above: no offer, no page, and no memo kept, so the PR is news again — once — when a push moves
                # the head past the verdict. See refused(); the alternative is telling a session, on a schedule
                # and in the box's own voice, to land a change the box itself said must not land.
                continue
            first = float(old.get(k) or 0) or t0          # first seen pending, carried across runs
            last, stood = float(old.get(ak) or 0), t0 - first    # `held` is taken above, and means something else
            keep[k] = first
            if stood >= pend_after and t0 - last >= pend_after:
                last = t0
                alert(f"⏳ {repo}/{track} — finished, PR open, unlanded for {stood / 3600:.0f}h — "
                      f"nobody has picked it up" + (f" · {f['pr']}" if f.get("pr") else ""))
            if last:
                keep[ak] = last
            if old.get(ok_):
                keep[ok_] = old[ok_]
            elif offer(repo, f"board watchdog: {repo}/{track}: finished, PR open, not landed — review and land it"
                             + (f" ({f['pr']})" if f.get("pr") else "")):
                keep[ok_] = t0                            # a refused offer (no session) was not heard: still owed
        if repos:   # a filtered run knows its repos only: the others' entries are not its to drop (the snapshot's rule above)
            keep.update({x: v for x, v in old.items() if x.split(": ")[0].removeprefix("pending ")
                         .removeprefix("alerted ").removeprefix("offered ").split("/")[0] not in repos})
        if keys or keep or old:   # only findings that are still true survive the write: one that comes back is news again
            ledger(path, dict({k: (t0 if k in told else old.get(k, 0)) for k in keys}, **keep))
        if fresh:
            # THE SESSION FIRST (owner, 2026-08-31, thread 1788215141): most board-vs-box drift — a finished
            # worker with no PR, a closed PR, a gone worktree — is the live planning session's to fix, not an
            # owner decision. Each finding is offered to that repo's session; the owner is paged only with what
            # no session took (`inject --no-start`: a dead daemon or no session window refuses, and THAT pages).
            # Tradeoff, named: a live session that drops an inject keeps the finding off the owner's phone for
            # the repeat window — cc-owed and the digest still carry the track meanwhile.
            paged = []
            for r in fresh:
                line = (f"board watchdog: {r['repo']}/{r['track']} — {r['why']}"
                        + (f" (set to {r['to']})" if r["applied"] else ""))
                if not offer(r["repo"], line):
                    paged.append(r)
            if paged:
                one = {r["repo"] for r in paged}
                title = f"{one.pop()} board" if len(one) == 1 else "board"   # cc-notify routes a title that starts with a repo name into that repo's updates lane
                body = "\n".join(f"• {r['repo']}/{r['track']} — {r['why']}"
                                 + (f" (set to {r['to']})" if r["applied"] else "") for r in paged)
                subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-notify", "-t", title,
                                f"The board did not match the box, and this part needs you:\n{body}"],
                               capture_output=True, timeout=30)
    return 1 if asks else 0


# ── selfcheck ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def selfcheck():
    """The decision table, then one end-to-end run against a throwaway HOME. No network, no Slack, no board
    but its own. Usage: cc-reconcile selfcheck"""
    import tempfile
    global BOARDS                          # declared once, up top: a later read of it must not predate this
    # cc-limit's resumed-episode file is pinned somewhere that does not exist, for the whole run: a real limit
    # that lifted this morning would otherwise decide whether a mark in a fixture below is cold.
    os.environ["CC_LIMIT_RESUMED"] = os.path.join(tempfile.gettempdir(), "cc-reconcile-selfcheck-no-episode")
    p = f = 0

    def ok(name, cond):
        nonlocal p, f
        if cond:
            p += 1
        else:
            f += 1
            print(f"  ✗ {name}")

    def F(**kw):
        d = {"status": "running", "pr": "", "pr_state": None, "live": False, "wt_exists": True,
             "age": 10 ** 6, "grace": GRACE, "journal": (None, ""), "loop_end": "",
             "commit_err": False, "push_err": False, "unpushed": True, "has_remote": True,
             "limited": False, "loops": [], "workers": [], "pane_idle": 0.0,
             "agent": "", "agent_live": False, "agent_stale": "", "held": "",
             "delivery": True, "started": False, "worked": False}   # every existing case below is a row that
             # really was dispatched through cc-task; the never-claimed, interactive-start and legacy-worker
             # ones are their own tests, below, and set these explicitly
        d.update(kw)
        return d

    def one(**kw):
        r = decide(F(**kw))
        return (r[0][0], r[0][1]) if r else ("none", None)

    def eff(**kw):                                      # the ONE word: what a consumer of the snapshot reads
        return effective(F(**kw), decide(F(**kw)))

    # a live process outranks the board, and a dead one never outranks a fresh dispatch
    ok("live worker in a blocked track → running", one(status="blocked", live=True) == ("fix", "running"))
    ok("live worker in a merged track is not un-merged", one(status="merged", live=True, pr="u", pr_state="MERGED") == ("none", None))
    ok("running + no worker, but dispatched seconds ago → left alone", one(age=5, grace=600) == ("none", None))
    # running with nobody working on it: the evidence decides which word replaces it
    ok("journal BLOCKED → waiting, and the owner is asked",
       decide(F(journal=("BLOCKED", "which one?")))[0][:2] == ("ask", "waiting"))
    ok("the blocked question is carried, not summarised", "which one?" in decide(F(journal=("BLOCKED", "which one?")))[0][2])
    ok("journal DONE + merged PR → merged", one(journal=("DONE", ""), pr="u", pr_state="MERGED") == ("fix", "merged"))
    ok("journal DONE + open PR → review", one(journal=("DONE", ""), pr="u", pr_state="OPEN") == ("fix", "review"))
    ok("journal DONE + no PR → asks (nobody opened one)", one(journal=("DONE", "")) == ("ask", "review"))
    ok("loop exit → blocked, with the loop's own reason", decide(F(loop_end="exit 4: stalled"))[0][:2] == ("ask", "blocked"))
    ok("nothing says why → blocked and the owner hears about it", one() == ("ask", "blocked"))
    # the PR outranks a stale status
    ok("review + merged PR → merged", one(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="MERGED") == ("fix", "merged"))
    ok("merged + open PR → review", one(status="merged", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN") == ("fix", "review"))
    ok("closed-not-merged PR is never guessed at", one(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="CLOSED") == ("ask", None))
    ok("an unreadable PR state is unknown, not a fix", one(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="?") == ("info", None))
    ok("no network → no PR verdict at all", one(status="review", pr="u", pr_state=None) == ("none", None))
    # what only a person can settle
    ok("a missing worktree is reported, not repaired",
       ("ask", None, "its worktree is gone") in decide(F(status="review", wt_exists=False)))
    # …UNLESS THE PR SAYS THE WORK WAS DONE SOMEWHERE ELSE: a planning session's own worktree, landed as a PR,
    # leaves a `review` row that never had a track worktree to lose. Both halves, and the rows still asked.
    ok("…but a `review` row whose PR is open is not asked about a worktree it never had",
       not any("worktree" in w for _, _, w in decide(F(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN", wt_exists=False))))
    ok("…while a PR number nobody could confirm silences nothing — only OPEN or MERGED says origin has the branch",
       all(("ask", None, "its worktree is gone") in decide(F(status="review", pr="u", pr_state=ps, wt_exists=False))
           for ps in (None, "?", "CLOSED")))
    ok("…and `running` and `waiting` are asked whatever their PR says — those words claim a worker is in one, so "
       "the word this run lands on must not silence the one the board still carries",
       all(("ask", None, "its worktree is gone") in decide(F(status=s_, pr="u", pr_state="OPEN", wt_exists=False))
           for s_ in ("running", "waiting")))
    ok("a merged track's missing worktree is normal", decide(F(status="merged", wt_exists=False)) == [])
    ok("a track that never started is not missing a worktree — under either word for it, since boards written before "
       "the board spoke `queued` still say `todo`",
       decide(F(status="queued", wt_exists=False)) == [] and decide(F(status="todo", wt_exists=False)) == []
       and decide(F(status="queued", live=True))[0][:2] == ("fix", "running"))
    ok("…AND SO IS ONE `word` MOVED OFF QUEUED BY HAND: a person or session can set any status straight onto a "
       "row (`cc-board status`/`note`), dispatch or not — lessons/tutor-on-a-member-lesson was `queued`, never "
       "claimed, only a task.md and no worktree ever, and a note marking it `blocked` (nothing wrong with "
       "starting) was read as a lost worktree. `delivery` is cc-task's claim record, not the board's word, and "
       "a row nothing ever claimed has none",
       decide(F(status="blocked", wt_exists=False, delivery=False)) == [])
    ok("…BUT A ROW cc-task DID CLAIM keeps being asked about, whatever word it wears now: the claim record "
       "outlives the worktree it was made for",
       ("ask", None, "its worktree is gone") in decide(F(status="blocked", wt_exists=False, delivery=True)))
    ok("…AND SO DOES ONE `cc <repo> <track>` STARTED INTERACTIVELY, EVEN WITH NO CLAIM ON FILE: __run/__runmember "
       "and cc-handoff set the board's `started` flag without ever calling `cc-task claim`, so `delivery` alone "
       "misses this dispatch path — `started` catches it",
       ("ask", None, "its worktree is gone") in decide(F(status="blocked", wt_exists=False, delivery=False, started=True)))
    ok("…AND SO DOES ONE THE LEGACY WORKER PATH RAN, EVEN WITH NEITHER A CLAIM NOR `started` ON FILE: `cc <repo> "
       "<track> --go` predates cc-task claim and never sets the board's `started` flag, but a row it actually "
       "ran left a progress.md or loop.log — `worked` catches it, and the never-claimed `blocked` case above "
       "stays empty",
       ("ask", None, "its worktree is gone") in decide(F(status="running", wt_exists=False, delivery=False, started=False, worked=True)))
    ok("a refused checkpoint is a finding", any("checkpoint" in w for _, _, w in decide(F(status="review", pr="u", commit_err=True))))
    # THE MARKER IS NOT THE ANSWER: `.cc/push.err` survives a push made any way but cc-checkpoint's own next one
    # (a person pushing after a refused lease), and one such file nagged a pushed worktree every 20 min for days.
    unp = lambda **kw: any("not pushed" in w for _, _, w in decide(F(status="review", pr="u", **kw)))
    ok("an unpushed branch is a finding: the marker AND commits origin does not have", unp(push_err=True, unpushed=True))
    ok("…but the marker alone is not — a worktree origin already has is not nagged, however old its push.err",
       not unp(push_err=True, unpushed=False))
    ok("…and neither is a branch with work origin lacks but no failed push to say so",
       not unp(push_err=False, unpushed=True))
    ok("…while a branch the box could not judge (never fetched, detached HEAD) keeps the marker's answer, because "
       "silence about a real unpushed branch is the worse of the two", unp(push_err=True, unpushed=None))
    # …AND WHOSE MARKER IT IS. For a member workspace the file in the worktree is the MEMBER's: their Stop hook has no
    # credential by design, so it writes push.err every turn, and the host writes its own state outside that tree, so
    # nothing clears it either side. Read here it made "its branch is not pushed" a finding every 20 min about a
    # branch the 5-minute sweep had pushed — and `branch_unpushed` cannot judge those branches (they push to a remote
    # named after the project, so `refs/remotes/origin/<branch>` never resolves and the answer is None, which keeps
    # the marker's word). The host's own marker is under ~/.cc/push-state (review of #225).
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tm:
        os.makedirs(f"{tm}/dev/ws/.cc"); open(f"{tm}/dev/ws/.cc/member-workspace", "w").close()
        ok("a member workspace's failed-push marker is the HOST's own, under ~/.cc/push-state — the file in the "
           "worktree is the member's, and nothing on the host writes or clears it",
           push_marker("ws", "t", tm) == f"{tm}/.cc/push-state/ws/t/push.err")
        ok("…while every other repo keeps it in the worktree, where the hook that writes it clears it",
           push_marker("plain", "t", tm) == f"{tm}/.cc/worktrees/plain/t/.cc/push.err")
        # …BUT THE REFUSED CHECKPOINT IS EITHER SIDE'S, because both sides refuse. `cc done` runs cc-checkpoint's
        # commit mode with the state dir, so the host's refusal is ~/.cc/push-state/<repo>/<track>/checkpoint.err —
        # read only in the worktree it lit `commit!` nowhere, and a `cc done` that refused to commit a staged secret
        # opened the PR with nothing said (round 11). The member's in-boundary Stop hook writes <wt>/.cc/checkpoint.err
        # and can write nowhere else — read only in push-state, a member session that stages .env.local is refused and
        # NOTHING says so anywhere, while the sweep skips the track because a refusal moves no HEAD (round 12).
        wtd, psd = f"{tm}/.cc/worktrees/ws/t/.cc", f"{tm}/.cc/push-state/ws/t"
        os.makedirs(wtd); os.makedirs(psd)
        def cm():   # the path AND whether it lit, read together: a path captured alone is falsified by the next rm
            p = commit_marker("ws", "t", tm)
            return p, os.path.exists(p)
        cm_none = cm()
        open(f"{wtd}/checkpoint.err", "w").close(); open(f"{wtd}/push.err", "w").close()   # the member's own two
        cm_member, pm_member = cm(), push_marker("ws", "t", tm)
        open(f"{psd}/checkpoint.err", "w").close()
        cm_both = cm()
        os.unlink(f"{wtd}/checkpoint.err")
        cm_host = cm()
        ok("a member workspace's `commit!` is lit by EITHER refusal — the host's under ~/.cc/push-state (what `cc "
           "done` writes) or the member's own in-boundary <wt>/.cc/checkpoint.err, the only place their hook can "
           "write; with neither there is nothing to light and the host's path is what a writer gets",
           cm_none == (f"{psd}/checkpoint.err", False) and cm_member == (f"{wtd}/checkpoint.err", True)
           and cm_both == (f"{psd}/checkpoint.err", True) and cm_host == (f"{psd}/checkpoint.err", True))
        ok("…while the failed push stays the HOST's alone, with the member's own push.err sitting right there and "
           "lighting nothing: theirs is credential-less by design, so it is rewritten every turn and cleared by "
           "nothing on the host, and reading it nagged for ever about a branch the sweep had pushed",
           pm_member == f"{psd}/push.err" and not os.path.exists(pm_member))
        ok("…and every other repo has one place for both, the worktree, where the hook that writes them clears them",
           commit_marker("plain", "t", tm) == f"{tm}/.cc/worktrees/plain/t/.cc/checkpoint.err")
    # A FINISHED ROW IS JUDGED AS FINISHED: cc-land deletes the branch on merge, so `origin/<branch>` is gone,
    # branch_unpushed() can only say None, and a push.err nothing clears made "its branch is not pushed" a finding
    # about a PR that HAD merged. Both halves, each on its own fixture — what is silenced, and what still fires.
    mrg = dict(status="merged", pr="u", pr_state="MERGED", push_err=True, unpushed=None, commit_err=True)
    ok("a merged row says nothing about its branch, its worktree or its last checkpoint: the merge deleted the "
       "branch, which is success", decide(F(**mrg)) == [] and decide(F(**dict(mrg, wt_exists=False))) == [])
    ok("…and `done` is the other finished word, with no PR to re-read and the same silence",
       decide(F(status="done", push_err=True, unpushed=None, commit_err=True, wt_exists=False)) == [])
    ok("…while a branch that is genuinely unpushed on a row nobody merged still says so",
       any("not pushed" in w for _, _, w in decide(F(status="running", live=True, push_err=True, unpushed=True))))
    # ONLY THE DOUBT IS FORGIVEN, NOT THE FACT. `unpushed=True` means origin/<branch> RESOLVED and HEAD was not
    # reachable from it — commits origin really lacks, which a merge cannot produce. Silencing that on a merged row
    # is how the work dies: it is committed, so not `dirty`, so cc-janitor rule 1 calls the worktree clean and
    # `cc rm`s it — `git branch -D` with the commits inside (review #203). Its own fixture, both words, both halves.
    for w_ in ("merged", "done"):
        ok(f"a `{w_}` row whose branch really does hold commits origin lacks is STILL told, though the same row is "
           "forgiven a branch the box could not judge — a fact is not a doubt",
           any("not pushed" in w for _, _, w in decide(F(status=w_, push_err=True, unpushed=True)))
           and not any("not pushed" in w for _, _, w in decide(F(status=w_, push_err=True, unpushed=None))))
    ok("…and that holds on the tick the PR merges too, when the row still says `review` and this run says `merged`",
       any("not pushed" in w for _, _, w in
           decide(F(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="MERGED", push_err=True, unpushed=True))))
    ok("…and the tick the PR merges is already finished: that run says `merged` and nothing about the branch",
       decide(F(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="MERGED", push_err=True, unpushed=None))
       == [("fix", "merged", "its PR merged")])
    ok("review with no PR and a remote → asks", any("never opened" in w for _, _, w in decide(F(status="review"))))
    ok("review with no PR and no remote is silent", decide(F(status="review", has_remote=False)) == [])
    ok("a quiet track says nothing", decide(F(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN")) == [])

    # WAITING — the row this tool was missing (owner, 2026-08-31). Alive is not the question; "does anything happen
    # without him" is. Every case here is about telling the three idle shapes apart.
    ok("a session ALIVE with a question for the owner is waiting, not running — the declaration outranks the pid, "
       "which is the whole bug: five of these said `running` for four hours",
       decide(F(status="running", live=True, journal=("BLOCKED", "which syllabus?")))[0][:2] == ("ask", "waiting"))
    ok("…and it is an ASK, so it reaches him, every run, for as long as the question stands",
       decide(F(status="waiting", live=True, journal=("BLOCKED", "which syllabus?")))[0][0] == "ask")
    ok("a worker BETWEEN ITERATIONS declared nothing and is simply running — not waiting on anyone",
       waits_for(F(live=True, loops=[2], workers=[1])) == ("", "")
       and decide(F(status="queued", live=True, loops=[2], workers=[1]))[0][:2] == ("fix", "running"))
    ok("a loop sitting out a usage limit waits on the CLOCK, not on a person, and never becomes an `ask`",
       waits_for(F(limited=True, loops=[2]))[0] == "clock"
       and [k for k, _, _ in decide(F(status="blocked", live=True, limited=True, loops=[2]))] == ["held"])
    ok("…and the two are told apart by WHO, not by the word `blocked`",
       waits_for(F(journal=("BLOCKED", "ship it?")))[0] == "person"
       and waits_for(F(limited=True, loops=[2]))[0] == "clock")
    ok("a limit standing over a track that is actually WORKING is not the clock's doing",
       waits_for(F(limited=True, loops=[2], workers=[1])) == ("", ""))
    ok("a stood-down track goes back to running by itself once its journal stops saying BLOCKED",
       decide(F(status="waiting", live=True))[0][:2] == ("fix", "running"))
    ok("…and with nobody in the worktree either, `waiting` is reconciled like a stale `running` is",
       decide(F(status="waiting", live=False))[0][:2] == ("ask", "blocked"))
    ok("`waiting` with a PR open is a person's word and survives --apply: reconciling it to `review` would erase it "
       "and the pending watchdog would nag on the next tick anyway (lessons#10; owner, 2026-09-01)",
       decide(F(status="waiting", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN")) == []
       and decide(F(status="waiting", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN", journal=("DONE", ""))) == []
       and decide(F(status="running", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN"))[0][:2] == ("fix", "review"))
    ok("…but the PR MERGING moves it: that is a fact, not a word — and a finish with no PR, or a PR closed "
       "without merging, is still reported, without touching the word",
       decide(F(status="waiting", pr="u", pr_state="MERGED", journal=("DONE", "")))[0][:2] == ("fix", "merged")
       and decide(F(status="waiting", journal=("DONE", "")))[0][:2] == ("ask", None)
       and decide(F(status="waiting", pr="u", pr_state="CLOSED", journal=("DONE", "")))[0][:2] == ("ask", None))
    ok("a merged track is never re-opened by an old question in its journal",
       decide(F(status="merged", journal=("BLOCKED", "old"), pr="u", pr_state="MERGED")) == [])
    ok("STATUS: BLOCKED with no question is still a declaration — it stopped, and only a person restarts it",
       decide(F(journal=("BLOCKED", "")))[0][:2] == ("ask", "waiting"))
    ok("nothing is ever inferred from prose: a journal that merely TALKS about waiting declares nothing",
       waits_for(F(journal=(None, "waiting on the owner for the syllabus"))) == ("", ""))
    # the snapshot: one decider, and a word for every track (rec #7)
    ok("the snapshot's word is what decide() would set, not what the board says",
       effective(F(status="running", live=True, journal=("BLOCKED", "q")),
                 decide(F(status="running", live=True, journal=("BLOCKED", "q")))) == "waiting")
    ok("…and the board's own word when nothing disagrees with it",
       effective(F(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN"), []) == "review")
    ok("the snapshot carries who and why, which one status word cannot",
       snapshot([("r", "t", F(status="running", live=True), "waiting", "person", "which syllabus?")])
       ["tracks"]["r/t"] == {"state": "waiting", "board": "running", "kind": "track", "live": True,
                             "waiting_on": "person", "why": "which syllabus?", "idle_for": 0,
                             "loop": False, "agent": False, "held": ""})
    ok("…and the three facts the Home tab and cc-pulse used to read for themselves: a live loop, a live agent mark, "
       "a person's hold — so neither needs a process table, a tmux or a board field of its own",
       {k: v for k, v in snapshot([("r", "t", F(status="queued", loops=[2], agent="@1:9", agent_live=True, held=" mine "),
                                     "running", "", "")])["tracks"]["r/t"].items() if k in ("loop", "agent", "held")}
       == {"loop": True, "agent": True, "held": "mine"})
    ok("`todo` is an older board's word for `queued`, and the snapshot says `queued` — one vocabulary for every reader",
       eff(status="todo") == "queued" and eff(status="queued") == "queued")

    # A SUBAGENT IS NOBODY TO /proc (owner, 2026-09-04: backlog rows run as a planning session's subagents). No
    # worker, no loop, no window of the track's own — so the row went to NEEDS YOU as "nothing says why" ten
    # minutes past the grace, and finding 4 of the review on #231 named the same gap.
    ok("a running row a LIVE session marked as its subagent's work is left alone past the grace, and raises "
       "no ask — the mark is the only thing on the box that says anybody is on it",
       decide(F(status="running", agent="@3:900", agent_live=True, age=10 ** 6)) == []
       and eff(status="running", agent="@3:900", agent_live=True) == "running")
    ok("…and the moment that session's window is gone the mark counts for nothing: the same row is reported "
       "abandoned, exactly as it was before any of this",
       decide(F(status="running", agent="@3:900", agent_live=False))[0][:2] == ("ask", "blocked"))
    ok("a marked row that is still `queued` or `blocked` is running, and says WHOSE — never 'a worker is in "
       "its worktree', which is the one thing that is not true of it",
       decide(F(status="queued", agent="@3:900", agent_live=True))[0] == ("fix", "running", "a live session is doing it in a subagent")
       and decide(F(status="blocked", agent="@3:900", agent_live=True))[0][:2] == ("fix", "running")
       and decide(F(status="queued", live=True))[0][2] == "a worker is in its worktree")
    ok("…AND IT HAS NO WORKTREE: nothing dispatched it, so there is none to be gone, and the mark has to silence "
       "that rule too — otherwise the row it just made quiet goes back to NEEDS YOU on the same run",
       decide(F(status="queued", agent="@3:900", agent_live=True, wt_exists=False))
       == [("fix", "running", "a live session is doing it in a subagent")]
       and decide(F(status="running", agent="@3:900", agent_live=True, wt_exists=False)) == [])
    ok("A COLD MARK IS REPORTED WITH ITS AGE, not as 'nothing says why': the row IS unattended, and the one "
       "thing worth saying about it is that a session took it 9 h ago and never came back",
       decide(F(status="running", agent="@3:900", agent_stale="marked by a live session 9 h ago, mark expired"))
       == [("ask", "blocked", "marked by a live session 9 h ago, mark expired")])
    ok("…AND IT SAYS IT ONCE. A row marked while `queued` was flipped to `running` by the mark, and that word "
       "outlives the mark: with the worktree rule guarded on a LIVE mark, the same row drew 'mark expired' AND "
       "'its worktree is gone' 6 h later — a second NEEDS YOU line for a row that had none before it was marked",
       decide(F(status="running", agent="@3:900", agent_live=False, wt_exists=False,
                agent_stale="marked by a live session 6 h ago, mark expired"))
       == [("ask", "blocked", "marked by a live session 6 h ago, mark expired")])
    # …and the weighing itself, which is what makes a mark cold. ALIVE IS NOT WORKING (owner, 2026-09-01): before
    # this, a marked row whose subagent died read `running` for as long as the session's window lived — the grace
    # rule, the worktree ask and cc-pulse's ready list all silent, and idle_session() unable to reach it, because
    # a marked row has no window of its own to be idle.
    W, T0 = {"@3:900": "r"}, 1000 * 3600
    def stamp(secs_ago): return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(T0 - secs_ago))
    ok("a mark made a minute ago holds the row, and one made past the bound does not — with its age in the "
       "reason, so the run says what happened rather than 'nothing says why'",
       mark_why({"agent": "@3:900", "agent_at": stamp(60)}, W, "r", T0, AGENT_MARK) == (True, "")
       and mark_why({"agent": "@3:900", "agent_at": stamp(9 * 3600)}, W, "r", T0, AGENT_MARK)
       == (False, "marked by a live session 9 h ago, mark expired"))
    ok("…and a mark with no time on it holds nothing, which is every mark written before this: unreadable is "
       "'long ago' here as everywhere else, and the row comes back rather than sitting in flight for ever",
       mark_why({"agent": "@3:900"}, W, "r", T0, AGENT_MARK)[0] is False
       and mark_why({"agent": "@3:900"}, W, "r", T0, AGENT_MARK)[1] == "marked by a live session, but the mark carries no time")
    # …AND A USAGE LIMIT ENDS A MARK BEFORE ITS TIME (2026-09-05): the limit killed the subagents mid-row and
    # their marks read warm for four hours after it lifted. Own fixture: `started` is passed in, so nothing on
    # the box and no case above decides these.
    _rz = T0 - 3600.0       # an episode that BEGAN an hour before this run
    ok("a mark made before a limit that has since lifted is cold whatever its age, and the reason names the "
       "hour so the row says what happened to it",
       mark_why({"agent": "@3:900", "agent_at": stamp(5400)}, W, "r", T0, AGENT_MARK, _rz)
       == (False, "marked before the usage limit that began at "
                  + time.strftime("%H:%MZ", time.gmtime(_rz)) + " — that subagent died with it"))
    # THE BAR IS THE START, NOT THE RESET (review of #264): cc-model puts the box on the fallback about a minute
    # into an episode and it goes on working, so a mark made after that is a subagent that is alive.
    ok("…while a mark made DURING the episode, once the box was on the fallback, holds its row: the box did not "
       "stop for the limit, and that subagent is still working",
       mark_why({"agent": "@3:900", "agent_at": stamp(1800)}, W, "r", T0, AGENT_MARK, _rz) == (True, ""))
    ok("…and one made at the START second does not: that is the second the limit was first seen in",
       mark_why({"agent": "@3:900", "agent_at": stamp(3600)}, W, "r", T0, AGENT_MARK, _rz)[0] is False)
    ok("…and with no episode passed in, the age bound is the whole rule, which is what this file did before",
       mark_why({"agent": "@3:900", "agent_at": stamp(5400)}, W, "r", T0, AGENT_MARK) == (True, ""))
    ok("…while a window that is gone, or is another repo's, is refused BEFORE the clock: there is no live "
       "session to name in the reason, and the row is reported exactly as any unattended row is",
       mark_why({"agent": "@9:900", "agent_at": stamp(60)}, W, "r", T0, AGENT_MARK) == (False, "")
       and mark_why({"agent": "@3:900", "agent_at": stamp(60)}, W, "other", T0, AGENT_MARK) == (False, ""))
    ok("the mark claims a session is alive and nothing else: a declared question still outranks it, and a "
       "merged row is still merged", decide(F(status="running", agent_live=True, journal=("BLOCKED", "q?")))[0][:2] == ("ask", "waiting")
       and decide(F(status="merged", agent_live=True, pr="u", pr_state="MERGED")) == [])
    ok("the snapshot counts a subagent as alive, so `cc ls` and the Home tab say the same thing this run did",
       snapshot([("r", "t", F(status="running", agent="@3:900", agent_live=True), "running", "", "")])
       ["tracks"]["r/t"]["live"] is True)
    # …and the mark itself: the pane listing is read in one pure pass, so the rule can be driven with no server.
    # 100 is a planning window with a session in it · 101 a track window with one · 104 a window with something
    # running but NO session (the `tail -f` somebody left) · 999 a pane whose shell has no child at all.
    P = ["proj\t100\t0\t@1:900", "proj/t1\t101\t0\t@2:900", "proj/t2\t999\t0\t@3:900",
         "proj\t102\t0\t", "other\t104\t0\t@4:900"]
    _live, _idle, _wins = panes(P, {"100", "101", "102", "104"}, {"100", "101"})
    ok("a window an `agent` mark can name is any window a SESSION is in — a planning session's is named for its "
       "repo alone, so the TRACK filter must not decide this, and the name comes back to be checked against it",
       _wins == {"@1:900": "proj", "@2:900": "proj/t1"} and _live == {"proj/t1"})
    # AN ORCHESTRATOR IS A SESSION OF THAT REPO. Its window is `<repo>@<alias>`, so plain equality called every
    # mark it made somebody else's and its rows were unattended from the first tick — and a handoff that carried
    # a predecessor's marks to an orch successor carried them where they stopped counting.
    _wo = {"@5:900": "proj@dash", "@6:900": "other@dash"}
    _at = {"agent_at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())}
    ok("an orchestrator's mark holds its own repo's row, and still holds no other repo's",
       mark_why(dict(_at, agent="@5:900"), _wo, "proj", now(), 6 * 3600)[0]
       and not mark_why(dict(_at, agent="@6:900"), _wo, "proj", now(), 6 * 3600)[0])
    # …AND AN ORCH'S WINDOW IS NOT IN TMUX SESSION `main`. It is grouped onto main under a session name of its
    # own — tmux answers `main-18` for its pane — and this file never asks a session's name at all, because
    # `list-panes -a` is every pane on the SERVER. Nothing held that, so a listing narrowed to one session
    # would pass every case above and drop every orch's mark on the real box. Own tmux server, on a socket
    # under $TMUX_TMPDIR with $TMUX unset (selftest.sh's pattern), so the box's own server is never touched —
    # and if anything already answers there, this touches nothing and fails rather than guessing.
    #
    # THE SOCKET IS NAMED TWICE, AND THE TEARDOWN IS A SESSION. tmux_live() is the code under test and shells out
    # to a bare `tmux`, so the ENV has to point it here; every call this fixture makes names that same path with
    # `-S` as well, so no later refactor of the env can aim one of them at the box's own server. And it ends with
    # `kill-session`: `kill-server` on the wrong socket takes every session on this box down with it, and being
    # right today only because two lines above popped $TMUX is not a thing to leave lying about.
    _started, _pid, _wid, _fw = False, "", "", {}
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as _ts:
        _sock = os.path.join(_ts, "tmux-%d" % os.getuid(), "default")   # where TMUX_TMPDIR=_ts resolves to
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(_sock), exist_ok=True)
        os.chmod(os.path.dirname(_sock), 0o700)         # tmux refuses a socket dir anyone else can read
        _keep = {k: os.environ.get(k) for k in ("TMUX", "TMUX_TMPDIR")}
        os.environ["TMUX_TMPDIR"] = _ts
        os.environ.pop("TMUX", None)
        def _tx(*a):
            try:
                return subprocess.run(["tmux", "-S", _sock, *a], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
            except Exception:                           # no tmux at all, or one that will not answer in time:
                return subprocess.CompletedProcess(a, 1, "", "")   # tmux_live swallows the same, and this case
        try:                                            # then goes RED with the rest — never a traceback
            if _tx("list-sessions").returncode != 0:    # nothing answers on our own socket: ours to make
                _started = _tx("-f", "/dev/null", "new-session", "-d", "-s", "notmain",
                               "-n", "proj@dash", "sleep 600").returncode == 0
            if _started:
                def _ask(fmt):
                    return _tx("display-message", "-p", "-t", "notmain:proj@dash", fmt).stdout.strip()
                _pid, _wid = _ask("#{pane_pid}"), _ask("#{window_id}:#{pid}")
                _fw = tmux_live({_pid}, {_pid})[2]
        finally:
            if _started:
                _tx("kill-session", "-t", "notmain")
            for _k, _v in _keep.items():
                os.environ.pop(_k, None) if _v is None else os.environ.update({_k: _v})
    ok("a real tmux window `<repo>@<alias>` in a session that is NOT `main` is read as that repo's, and its "
       "mark holds the row — an orch lives in a session of its own and every one of its rows turns on this",
       _started and _fw.get(_wid) == "proj@dash"
       and mark_why(dict(_at, agent=_wid), _fw, "proj", now(), 6 * 3600) == (True, "")
       and mark_why(dict(_at, agent=_wid), _fw, "other", now(), 6 * 3600) == (False, ""))
    ok("A CHILD IN THE PANE IS NOT A SESSION: a window left with a `tail -f` or an `htop` in it after the session "
       "exited has one for ever, and a mark on it would keep a row alive for ever — it is live for the TRACK "
       "rule and unusable as a mark, which is the difference the two tests exist for",
       "@4:900" not in _wins and panes(P, {"104"}, {"104"})[2] == {"@4:900": "other"})
    ok("…and a pane whose shell has no child is a leftover window, in neither: `cc` leaves one behind an exited "
       "session (exec bash)", "@3:900" not in _wins and "proj/t2" not in _live)
    ok("the mark carries the tmux SERVER too, because window ids restart at @0 when the server does — a mark "
       "left by a session that died in a reboot must not match whatever window is @1 now",
       "@1:900" in _wins and "@1:901" not in _wins)
    # THROUGH facts(), off a board FILE, so the field name itself is under test and not just decide()'s reading
    # of a fact handed to it. procs() and tmux_live() are stood in for; everything between them is the real path.
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tb:
        _B, _W, _pr, _tl = BOARDS, WTS, procs, tmux_live
        globals().update(BOARDS=tb, WTS=tb,
                         procs=lambda: (set(), set(), {"7"}, {}, {}),
                         tmux_live=lambda ppids, sessions=(): (
                             set(), {}, {"@5:900": "agentrepo", "@6:900": "otherrepo"}))
        try:
            # WTS. t1 AND t4 GET NO WORKTREE ON PURPOSE: nothing dispatches a row a session works in its
            # subagents, so that is the shape the real row has, warm mark (t1) or cold (t4), and a fixture that
            # made one for them is what hid the missing-worktree finding through two rounds of #242's review.
            # t2 and t3 are unmarked rows that really were dispatched, and keep theirs.
            for tr in ("t2", "t3"):
                os.makedirs(f"{tb}/agentrepo/{tr}")
            _fresh = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(time.time() - 60))
            json.dump({"repo": "agentrepo", "tracks": {
                "t1": {"status": "running", "agent": "@5:900", "agent_at": _fresh, "updated": "2020-01-01T00:00:00Z"},
                "t2": {"status": "running", "agent": "@6:900", "agent_at": _fresh, "updated": "2020-01-01T00:00:00Z"},
                "t3": {"status": "running", "updated": "2020-01-01T00:00:00Z"},
                "t4": {"status": "running", "agent": "@5:900", "agent_at": "2020-01-01T00:00:00Z",
                       "updated": "2020-01-01T00:00:00Z"}}},
                open(f"{tb}/agentrepo.json", "w"))
            got = {tr: fa for _, tr, _, fa in facts(["agentrepo"], net=False)}
        finally:
            globals().update(BOARDS=_B, WTS=_W, procs=_pr, tmux_live=_tl)
        ok("facts() reads the mark off the board file and calls the row alive, worktree or none — the whole "
           "path, so the field name is under test too and no fixture stands in for the missing worktree",
           got["t1"]["agent"] == "@5:900" and got["t1"]["agent_live"] is True
           and got["t1"]["wt_exists"] is False and decide(got["t1"]) == [])
        ok("A SESSION SPEAKS FOR ITS OWN REPO: a mark naming a window that belongs to another repo holds nothing "
           "open — the board is one file every session can write, and this is the reach it must not give",
           got["t2"]["agent_live"] is False and decide(got["t2"])[0][:2] == ("ask", "blocked"))
        ok("…and an unmarked row is untouched by any of it",
           got["t3"]["agent"] == "" and got["t3"]["agent_live"] is False
           and decide(got["t3"])[0][:2] == ("ask", "blocked"))
        ok("A MARK GOES COLD, and facts() carries the age off the board file to say so — t4's session is the "
           "same live one holding t1 open, and the only difference between the two rows is the clock. ONE "
           "record, not two: the row still has no worktree, and a cold mark must not turn that into a second "
           "finding the row never drew before it was marked",
           got["t4"]["agent_live"] is False and got["t4"]["wt_exists"] is False
           and len(decide(got["t4"])) == 1
           and decide(got["t4"])[0][:2] == ("ask", "blocked")
           and decide(got["t4"])[0][2].endswith("h ago, mark expired"))

    # IDLE — a session open at its prompt is not a session working (owner, 2026-09-01)
    ok("a live pane idle 3 h is `idle`, not running: nothing is happening at it",
       eff(status="running", live=True, pane_idle=3 * 3600) == "idle")
    ok("…and the snapshot says how long, so the tab can print it instead of a stale iteration count",
       snapshot([("r", "t", F(status="running", live=True, pane_idle=3 * 3600), "idle", "", "")])
       ["tracks"]["r/t"]["idle_for"] == 3 * 3600)
    ok("a pane touched 10 min ago is simply running",
       eff(status="running", live=True, pane_idle=600) == "running")
    ok("a worker or a loop alive is work in flight whatever the keyboard shows",
       not idle_session(F(live=True, workers=[1], pane_idle=10 ** 5))
       and not idle_session(F(live=True, loops=[1], pane_idle=10 ** 5)))
    ok("no activity stamp is not evidence of idleness",
       not idle_session(F(live=True)) and eff(status="running", live=True) == "running")
    ok("`idle` never touches what counts as WAITING: a declared question still outranks the keyboard",
       eff(status="running", live=True, pane_idle=10 ** 5, journal=("BLOCKED", "q")) == "waiting")
    ok("…nor what the tool STOPS: an orphan idle at its prompt is still an orphan",
       eff(status="running", live=True, pane_idle=10 ** 5, workers=[1], stop_why="orphan") == "blocked")

    # PENDING — finished, PR open, and nobody landed it. Every row here is CONSISTENT: decide() has nothing to
    # say about any of them, which is exactly why four of them sat for a day (owner, 2026-09-01).
    ok("finished with an open PR and nobody landing it is pending",
       pending(F(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN"), eff(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN"))
       and decide(F(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN")) == [])
    ok("an unreadable PR state never nags — `?` is not evidence that work is sitting",
       not pending(F(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="?"), eff(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="?")))
    ok("…nor is a run that never asked: --no-net leaves no PR fact, so nothing nags",
       not pending(F(status="review", pr="u", pr_state=None), eff(status="review", pr="u", pr_state=None)))
    ok("a landed track is not pending", not pending(F(status="merged", pr="u", pr_state="MERGED"), "merged"))
    ok("a track still being worked is not pending",
       not pending(F(status="running", live=True, pr="u", pr_state="OPEN"),
                   eff(status="running", live=True, pr="u", pr_state="OPEN")))
    ok("it is the EFFECTIVE state that counts, not the board's word: a stale `running` over an open PR with "
       "nobody in the worktree is a finished track, and pending",
       eff(status="running", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN") == "review"
       and pending(F(status="running", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN"), eff(status="running", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN")))
    # HELD — a person stopped this row and said why (cc-board's `held`). Every case is the SAME fixture twice, the
    # word on and the word off, because the whole claim is that the hold is what silences it.
    ok("a held row raises nothing a person has to answer: the row that would be reported abandoned says `held` "
       "instead, and the same row unheld is the `ask` it always was",
       [k for k, _, _ in decide(F(status="running", live=False, held="behind #309"))] == ["held"]
       and decide(F(status="running", live=False))[0][:2] == ("ask", "blocked"))
    ok("…and it takes a REASON to hold one: an empty word, or one that is only spaces, holds nothing",
       decide(F(status="running", live=False, held=""))[0][0] == "ask"
       and decide(F(status="running", live=False, held="   "))[0][0] == "ask")
    ok("…the hold carries its reason into what is printed, because a hold nobody can see is the board note this "
       "replaces", decide(F(status="running", live=False, held="behind #309"))[0][2] == "held: behind #309")
    ok("a held row is still RECONCILED — a silent fix keeps the board true about a merged PR, and `held` says so "
       "beside it; the ask is the only thing the hold takes away",
       [(k, s) for k, s, _ in decide(F(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="MERGED", held="behind #309"))]
       == [("fix", "merged"), ("held", None)]
       and eff(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="MERGED", held="behind #309") == "merged")
    ok("…and a RUNAWAY on a held row is still stopped: a hold is a person's word about what to do next, never a "
       "licence to burn tokens with nothing coming out",
       [k for k, _, _ in decide(F(status="running", live=True, held="behind #309", stop_why="orphan"))]
       == ["stop", "held"])
    # …AND NEITHER IS THE HOST'S OWN WORD. A hold silences the box's guess at a next move, nothing more: `held` is
    # member-writable on a member workspace board, so anything it could mute is something a member could turn off
    # for their own row.
    ok("…and a held row whose branch is not pushed still says so: cc-janitor deletes that branch at 48 h, and a "
       "hold that muted this would feed the only copy of those commits to `git branch -D`",
       any("not pushed" in w for _, _, w in
           decide(F(status="running", live=False, held="behind #309", push_err=True, unpushed=True)))
       and any("only in the worktree" in w for _, _, w in
               decide(F(status="running", live=False, held="behind #309", commit_err=True))))
    ok("…and a held row's QUESTION still reaches the owner: the person-ask is what puts the row on `waiting`, his "
       "NEEDS YOU list, and a hold is a word about the next move, not an answer to what was asked",
       [(k, s) for k, s, _ in decide(F(status="running", live=True, held="behind #309",
                                       journal=("BLOCKED", "which repo?")))]
       == [("ask", "waiting"), ("held", None)]
       and eff(status="running", live=True, held="behind #309", journal=("BLOCKED", "which repo?")) == "waiting")
    ok("…while a SESSION row is untouched by any of it: a channel is not a task, so it has nothing to hold",
       decide(F(kind="session", status="running", live=True, held="behind #309")) == [])
    ok("…except the one word a PERSON typed: a `waiting` row is never pending, however it is reconciled — landing "
       "is what it is waiting on (lessons#10 was nagged five times; owner, 2026-09-01)",
       not pending(F(status="waiting", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN"), "review")
       and not pending(F(status="waiting", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN"),
                       eff(status="waiting", pr="u", pr_state="OPEN")))

    # the runaway cc-loop cannot catch: its own. Both shapes are ALIVE, which is why nothing else here sees them
    def W(**kw):
        d = {"orphans": [], "workers": [111], "loops": [222], "limited": False, "idle": 4 * 3600}
        d.update(kw)
        return d

    ok("an orphaned worker — no cc-loop above it — is ended on sight, first sighting and all",
       "no cc-loop above it" in runaway(W(orphans=[111], loops=[]), False))
    ok("a worker under its loop, moving, is left alone", runaway(W(idle=60), True) == "")
    ok("wedged for hours but seen only once → not yet; one run is not two",
       runaway(W(), False) == "")
    ok("wedged for hours and the run before saw it too → ended", "wedged" in runaway(W(), True))
    ok("a loop waiting out a usage limit is waiting, not wedged", runaway(W(limited=True), True) == "")
    # the stamp reader itself: one run's limit is not the box's (review of #215 — a one-observer stamp silenced
    # the wedged alarm for every other track and filed them under "clock")
    import tempfile
    _keep, _p = os.environ.get("CC_LIMIT_STAMP"), tempfile.mkstemp(suffix=".claude-limit")[1]
    os.environ["CC_LIMIT_STAMP"] = _p
    try:
        _t = int(now()) + 3600
        open(_p, "w").write(f"{_t}\tusage limit\t{int(now())}\tclaude-fable-5-1\tdeadbeef\n")
        ok("a stamp ONE run hit leaves the reconciler unlimited: that run's limit is not the box's",
           not limit_active() and limit_until() == 0.0)
        open(_p, "w").write(f"{_t}\tusage limit\t{int(now())}\t\tdeadbeef,cafef00d\n")
        ok("two runs on it make it the box's — with an empty model field, as the 09-04 stamp had",
           limit_active() and limit_until() == float(_t))
    finally:
        os.unlink(_p)
        os.environ.pop("CC_LIMIT_STAMP", None) if _keep is None else os.environ.update({"CC_LIMIT_STAMP": _keep})
    ok("a loop between iterations, with no worker under it, is not wedged", runaway(W(workers=[]), True) == "")
    ok("an expensive job that keeps moving is never a runaway — there is no spend cap anywhere here",
       runaway(W(idle=30), True) == "")
    ok("…and decide() ends it instead of calling it healthy",
       decide(F(status="running", live=True, stop_why="x"))[0][:2] == ("stop", "blocked"))
    ok("a track being ended is not also 'fixed' to running",
       len(decide(F(status="queued", live=True, stop_why="x"))) == 1)
    # KIND=SESSION — a channel, not a task (owner, 2026-09-01): every LIVENESS row of the table above is refused for
    # one; the owner's question is the one row it keeps (review of #132), and its one word is its kind
    ok("a session row is never moved by presence, grace, a PR, a worktree, DONE or a runaway — alive, dead past "
       "grace, merged, wedged: decide() has nothing to say; the same facts on a track still decide it",
       all(decide(F(kind="session", **kw)) == [] for kw in (
           dict(status="running"), dict(status="blocked", live=True), dict(status="queued", live=True),
           dict(status="review", pr="u", pr_state="MERGED"), dict(status="running", live=True, stop_why="x"),
           dict(status="running", wt_exists=False, push_err=True), dict(status="running", journal=("DONE", ""))))
       and decide(F(kind="track", status="blocked", live=True))[0][:2] == ("fix", "running")
       and decide(F(status="blocked", live=True))[0][:2] == ("fix", "running"))
    ok("…but a session with a question for the owner is WAITING like a track — the ask record, so it is set, offered "
       "and paged: five course sessions are what waits_for() was written for, and kind=session must not silence them",
       decide(F(kind="session", status="running", live=True, journal=("BLOCKED", "which syllabus?")))[0][:2] == ("ask", "waiting")
       and "which syllabus?" in decide(F(kind="session", status="running", live=True, journal=("BLOCKED", "which syllabus?")))[0][2]
       and decide(F(kind="session", status="running", live=False, journal=("BLOCKED", "which syllabus?")))[0][:2] == ("ask", "waiting")
       and decide(F(kind="session", status="merged", journal=("BLOCKED", "old"))) == [])
    ok("…and it clears the same way once the journal stops saying BLOCKED — never to the `blocked` a dead track "
       "gets. The word it lands on is one the box can SEE: `running` where a session is open, `queued` where the "
       "window is gone, because `running` is a claim that something is happening and nothing is (#174 stopped the "
       "DISPLAY printing it; this is the board field, which was flipped back on every tick of a dead session)",
       decide(F(kind="session", status="waiting", live=True))[0][:2] == ("fix", "running")
       and decide(F(kind="session", status="waiting", live=False))[0][:2] == ("fix", "queued")
       and decide(F(status="waiting", live=False))[0][:2] == ("ask", "blocked"))
    ok("the ONE WORD for a session is its kind, `session` — cc ls and cc digest print that, never the `running` its "
       "row carries — with `waiting` the one track word it wears; a track's word is unchanged",
       effective(F(kind="session", status="running"), []) == "session"
       and effective(F(kind="session", status="waiting"), [("fix", "running", "")]) == "session"
       and effective(F(kind="session", status="running"), [("ask", "waiting", "")]) == "waiting"
       and effective(F(kind="session", status="waiting"), [("fix", "queued", "")]) == "session"   # …and the dead one reads the same
       and effective(F(status="running"), []) == "running" and effective(F(status="waiting"), [("fix", "running", "")]) == "running")

    # branch_unpushed on REAL git, because the fixture above only proves decide() reads the fact it is given
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tg:
        def g(d, *a):
            return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", d, *a], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
        bare, wt2 = f"{tg}/origin.git", f"{tg}/wt"
        g(tg, "init", "-q", "--bare", bare)
        g(tg, "clone", "-q", bare, wt2)
        g(wt2, "config", "user.email", "s@e"); g(wt2, "config", "user.name", "s")
        open(f"{wt2}/a", "w").write("1")
        g(wt2, "add", "a"); g(wt2, "commit", "-qm", "one")
        ok("real git: a branch origin has never seen cannot be judged — None, so the marker's answer stands",
           branch_unpushed(wt2) is None)
        g(wt2, "push", "-q", "-u", "origin", "HEAD")
        ok("real git: a branch origin HAS is not unpushed, whatever a leftover push.err says",
           branch_unpushed(wt2) is False)
        open(f"{wt2}/a", "w").write("2")
        g(wt2, "commit", "-qam", "two")
        ok("real git: a commit origin does not have IS unpushed", branch_unpushed(wt2) is True)
        ok("real git: a directory that is not a checkout cannot be judged", branch_unpushed(tg + "/nope") is None)

    # end to end: a real board, a real worktree, a real apply — in a HOME of its own
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as th:
        env = dict(os.environ, HOME=th, CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY="1", CC_NOTIFY_LOG=f"{th}/notify.log")
        # Named for THIS process. landing() reads the box-wide process table, and the lander fixture below is a
        # real `cc-land <repo> 9` argv: under a constant name a second copy of this selfcheck running at the same
        # time (two landings' suites, two worktrees) saw the first one's lander and lost the "news again" case —
        # 1 red in 6 concurrent runs, 2026-09-02, and the one reason ~/.cc/selftest.lock was still held.
        repo = f"selfcheckrepo{os.getpid()}"
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/{repo}/t1", exist_ok=True)
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/{repo}/t3", exist_ok=True)
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/state/{repo}/t1", exist_ok=True)
        open(f"{th}/.cc/state/{repo}/t1/progress.md", "w").write("- did a thing\nSTATUS: BLOCKED: which schema?\n")

        def cb(*a):
            return subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-board", *a], env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)

        cb("init", repo, f"{th}/dev/{repo}", "main")
        cb("add", repo, "t1", "a track that stood down", "")
        cb("status", repo, "t1", "running")
        r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), repo, "--apply", "--notify",
                            "--no-net", "--grace", "0"], env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: exits 1 when something needs a person", r.returncode == 1)
        ok("end to end: the track that stood down says `waiting` on the board now — not `running`, and not the "
           "`blocked` that reads the same as a loop that gave up",
           cb("get", repo, "t1", "status").stdout.strip() == "waiting")
        ok("end to end: the board carries a note saying it was reconciled",
           any("reconciled: running → waiting" in n["msg"]
               for n in json.loads(cb("json", repo).stdout)["tracks"]["t1"]["notes"]))
        ok("end to end: the question from the journal reached the report", "which schema?" in r.stdout)
        ok("end to end: the orphaned worktree is reported", f"{repo}/t3" in r.stdout)
        ok("end to end: the owner was told once", sum("board" in ln for ln in
                                                      open(f"{th}/notify.log").read().splitlines()) == 1)
        r2 = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), repo, "--apply", "--notify",
                             "--no-net", "--grace", "0"], env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: a second run does not page the owner again",
           sum("board" in ln for ln in open(f"{th}/notify.log").read().splitlines()) == 1)
        ok("end to end: and it is idempotent — the track stays waiting",
           cb("get", repo, "t1", "status").stdout.strip() == "waiting" and r2.returncode == 1)
        # the session-first offer: a finding a live session ACCEPTS pages nobody; one nobody takes still pages.
        argvN = [sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), repo, "--apply", "--notify",
                 "--no-net", "--grace", "0", "--repeat", "0"]
        base = sum("board" in ln for ln in open(f"{th}/notify.log").read().splitlines())
        subprocess.run(argvN, env=dict(env, CC_RECONCILE_INJECT="/bin/true"),
                       capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: a finding the live session accepts pages nobody",
           sum("board" in ln for ln in open(f"{th}/notify.log").read().splitlines()) == base)
        subprocess.run(argvN, env=dict(env, CC_RECONCILE_INJECT="/bin/false"),
                       capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: …and one nobody took still pages the owner",
           sum("board" in ln for ln in open(f"{th}/notify.log").read().splitlines()) == base + 1)
        def tree(root):   # every file under it with its size and mtime: what a pure read must leave exactly as it found
            out = {}
            for d, _, fs in os.walk(root):
                for x in fs:
                    try:
                        st_ = os.stat(f"{d}/{x}"); out[f"{d}/{x}"] = (st_.st_size, st_.st_mtime_ns)
                    except OSError:
                        pass
            return out
        before = tree(f"{th}/.cc")
        r3 = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "snapshot"],
                            env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        snp = json.loads(r3.stdout)
        ok("end to end: `snapshot` prints every track's real state, who it waits on and why",
           snp["tracks"][f"{repo}/t1"]["state"] == "waiting"
           and snp["tracks"][f"{repo}/t1"]["waiting_on"] == "person"
           and "which schema?" in snp["tracks"][f"{repo}/t1"]["why"])
        ok("end to end: …and WRITES NOTHING — no reconcile.json, no wedged ledger, no sweep's leftovers: the Home tab "
           "and cc-pulse take one per pass, and a pass must never move the timer's state",
           r3.returncode == 0 and not os.path.exists(f"{th}/.cc/state/reconcile.json") and tree(f"{th}/.cc") == before)
        rA = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "snapshot", "--apply"],
                            env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: …and refuses --apply and --notify outright, rather than quietly doing either",
           rA.returncode == 2 and "writes nothing" in rA.stderr and tree(f"{th}/.cc") == before)
        subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "--no-net", "--grace", "0"],
                       env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)   # the timer's shape: unfiltered, so it writes
        ok("end to end: the unfiltered run is what leaves it on disk for cc-digest and `cc ls` to read",
           json.load(open(f"{th}/.cc/state/reconcile.json"))["tracks"][f"{repo}/t1"]["state"] == "waiting")
        view_ = lambda: subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "view"], env=env,
                                       capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        rv = view_(); row = [ln.split("\t") for ln in rv.stdout.splitlines() if ln.startswith(f"{repo}/t1\t")]
        ok("end to end: `view` hands the readers that row as TSV — key, state, who, why — from the file, not a rerun",
           rv.returncode == 0 and len(row) == 1 and row[0][1:3] == ["waiting", "person"] and "which schema?" in row[0][3])
        sp = f"{th}/.cc/state/reconcile.json"; fresh = open(sp).read()
        open(sp, "w").write(json.dumps(dict(json.loads(fresh), epoch=int(now()) - FRESH - 1))); rv2 = view_()
        open(sp, "w").write(fresh)
        ok("end to end: …and a stale one is no rows at all, exit 0 — the reader keeps the board's word",
           rv2.returncode == 0 and rv2.stdout == "")
        # a SESSION row beside it: nobody in its worktree, past every grace, a question in its journal. The question
        # is the owner's like a track's — `waiting`, reported, in the snapshot with who and why, paged when no session
        # took it (review of #132) — but answered with nobody on it, the row goes to `queued`, not to the `running`
        # it used to be flipped back to on every tick and not to the `blocked` a dead track is reconciled to
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/{repo}/sess9", exist_ok=True)
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/state/{repo}/sess9", exist_ok=True)
        open(f"{th}/.cc/state/{repo}/sess9/progress.md", "w").write("- recon done\nSTATUS: BLOCKED: which syllabus?\n")
        cb("add", repo, "sess9", "a course session", "")
        cb("set", repo, "sess9", "kind", "session")
        cb("status", repo, "sess9", "running")
        rS = subprocess.run(argvN, env=dict(env, CC_RECONCILE_INJECT="/bin/false"), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        snap_ = lambda: json.loads(subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "snapshot"], env=env,
                                                  capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120).stdout)["tracks"]
        snpS = snap_().get(f"{repo}/sess9") or {}   # `snapshot` is pure: it is the box as it stands, not the file
        ok("end to end: a session's question reaches the owner like a track's — `waiting` on the board, in the run's "
           "output, in the snapshot with who and why, and paged when no session took it",
           cb("get", repo, "sess9", "status").stdout.strip() == "waiting" and "sess9" in rS.stdout
           and snpS.get("state") == "waiting" and snpS.get("waiting_on") == "person" and "which syllabus?" in snpS.get("why", "")
           and "sess9" in open(f"{th}/notify.log").read())
        open(f"{th}/.cc/state/{repo}/sess9/progress.md", "w").write("- owner answered; building\n")
        subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), repo, "--apply", "--notify",
                        "--no-net", "--grace", "0"], env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        snpS = snap_()[f"{repo}/sess9"]
        ok("end to end: …and answered, it leaves `waiting` — never to the `blocked` a dead track gets, and with no "
           "session open on it, never to `running` either: the board field says `queued` and the snapshot word is "
           "`session`, so cc ls and cc digest print neither",
           cb("get", repo, "sess9", "status").stdout.strip() == "queued"
           and snpS["state"] == "session" and snpS["waiting_on"] == "")
        # the other half of waiting: it must CLEAR. The question is gone from the journal and nobody is in the
        # worktree, so the next apply reconciles the stale word instead of leaving `waiting` stuck on the board.
        # (With someone live it goes back to `running` — that leg is a decide() row above; liveness cannot be
        # faked from here without a real process, so end to end covers the dead-worktree half.)
        open(f"{th}/.cc/state/{repo}/t1/progress.md", "w").write("- owner answered; picked back up\n")
        r4 = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), repo, "--apply", "--notify",
                             "--no-net", "--grace", "0"], env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: an answered question does not leave `waiting` stuck on the board",
           cb("get", repo, "t1", "status").stdout.strip() == "blocked")
        # PENDING end to end: finished, PR open, nobody landing it. The only way in is a real PR answer, so `gh`
        # is faked on PATH — this stays a no-network suite — and the inject is a recorder, since what is being
        # pinned is that the offer repeats rather than whether one was taken.
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/{repo}/t2", exist_ok=True)
        open(f"{th}/gh", "w").write("#!/bin/sh\necho OPEN\n")
        open(f"{th}/inj", "w").write(f'#!/bin/sh\necho "$@" >> {th}/inj.log\n')
        os.chmod(f"{th}/gh", 0o755)
        os.chmod(f"{th}/inj", 0o755)
        open(f"{th}/inj.log", "w").close()
        cb("add", repo, "t2", "finished, PR open, nobody landed it", "")
        cb("status", repo, "t2", "review")
        cb("set", repo, "t2", "pr", "https://example.invalid/pr/9")
        argvP = [sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), repo, "--apply", "--notify", "--grace", "0"]
        penv = dict(env, PATH=f"{th}:{os.environ['PATH']}", CC_RECONCILE_INJECT=f"{th}/inj")
        seenp = f"{th}/.cc/state/reconcile.seen.json"

        def offered():
            return sum("not landed" in ln for ln in open(f"{th}/inj.log").read().splitlines())

        def alerted():
            return sum(ln.startswith("alerts") for ln in open(f"{th}/notify.log").read().splitlines())

        a0 = alerted()
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: a finished track with an open PR is handed to the repo's own session", offered() == 1)
        ok("end to end: …and NOT to the owner yet — it has only just finished", alerted() == a0)
        ok("end to end: the pending key survives the ledger write, which used to keep `ask` findings only",
           float(json.load(open(seenp))[f"pending {repo}/t2"]) > 0)
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: the offer is made ONCE per PR — the next run, nothing changed, says nothing to the session",
           offered() == 1)
        led = json.load(open(seenp))                     # …and now it has stood there for five hours
        led[f"pending {repo}/t2"] = now() - 5 * 3600
        json.dump(led, open(seenp, "w"))
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: past the window nobody picked it up, so #alerts gets one line", alerted() == a0 + 1)
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: …one per window, however many times the timer fires in it — and still one offer",
           alerted() == a0 + 1 and offered() == 1)
        os.remove(f"{th}/gh")                            # no gh = no PR answer at all, which is not evidence
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: with the PR state unreadable it goes quiet rather than guessing at unlanded work",
           offered() == 1)
        ok("end to end: …and keeps the keys: one failed poll restarts neither the owner's clock nor the offer",
           all(x in json.load(open(seenp)) for x in (f"pending {repo}/t2", f"alerted {repo}/t2",
                                                       f"offered {repo}/t2 https://example.invalid/pr/9")))
        open(f"{th}/gh", "w").write("#!/bin/sh\necho OPEN\n")
        os.chmod(f"{th}/gh", 0o755)
        # the two rows the watchdog leaves alone. `waiting` is the board's own word for "the owner decides", and in
        # a repo where merging is owner-gated that IS the landing (lessons#10 was told five times, 2026-09-01) —
        # the ask path carries it. A PR somebody is landing is named by a job file, or by a live cc-land argv.
        # …on a FRESH ledger, so the case asserts the skip and not the once-per-PR key that would suppress the
        # offer anyway (review of #150): without the rule this run offers a second time.
        json.dump({}, open(seenp, "w"))
        cb("status", repo, "t2", "waiting")
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: a row the board says is `waiting` is not nagged about its PR", offered() == 1)
        ok("end to end: …and --apply leaves that word alone, so the skip holds on every tick, not just the first",
           cb("get", repo, "t2", "status").stdout.strip() == "waiting")
        # the control: same clean ledger, same open PR, only the word differs — this one IS nagged, once
        cb("status", repo, "t2", "review")
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: …the same row back in `review` is offered once — the ledger was not what silenced it",
           offered() == 2)
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/state/land", exist_ok=True)
        json.dump({"repo": repo, "pr": 9}, open(f"{th}/.cc/state/land/{repo}-9.json", "w"))
        json.dump({}, open(seenp, "w"))                  # a clean ledger: only the job file can silence this run
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: a PR with a job in the landing queue is being landed — no nag", offered() == 2)
        os.remove(f"{th}/.cc/state/land/{repo}-9.json")
        open(f"{th}/cc-land", "w").write("#!/bin/sh\nsleep 60\n")   # a live argv naming the PR, not a real landing
        os.chmod(f"{th}/cc-land", 0o755)
        lander = subprocess.Popen([f"{th}/cc-land", repo, "9"], start_new_session=True)
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        lander.kill()
        lander.wait(timeout=10)
        ok("end to end: a PR named by a live cc-land argv is being landed — no nag", offered() == 2)
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: …and one that falls out of a lane unlanded is news again, once", offered() == 3)
        # …and the gap BETWEEN two rounds, which is where the nag actually got out (PR #421, 2026-09-11): the round
        # ended at 10:47Z and took its job file with it, the next was queued at 10:53Z, and the tick fell between
        # them. cc-land's work record is the one thing that stands through that gap.
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/state/land/root", exist_ok=True)
        rootp = f"{th}/.cc/state/land/root/{repo}-9.json"
        json.dump({"repo": repo, "pr": 9, "reviews": 2, "repairs": 1}, open(rootp, "w"))
        json.dump({}, open(seenp, "w"))                  # a clean ledger: only the record can silence this run
        o_ = offered()
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: a PR between two landing rounds — no job file, but a work record a landing wrote minutes "
           "ago — is mid-flight, not abandoned: no nag", offered() == o_)
        cold = now() - BETWEEN_ROUNDS - 60
        os.utime(rootp, (cold, cold))
        json.dump({}, open(seenp, "w"))
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: …and a record older than that is history, not a landing: the same row is nagged once",
           offered() == o_ + 1)
        os.remove(rootp)

        # A PARKED PROJECT IS NOT NAGGED — not its session, not the owner. The REAL cc-pause is asked here, reading
        # the real flag file in this scratch HOME, so the whole path is pinned rather than a stand-in for it. Its own
        # fixture: a clean ledger, so nothing but the pause can be the reason the offer does not happen.
        PAUSED_FLAG = '{"at":"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z","by":"owner","note":"","loops":[]}\n'
        json.dump({}, open(seenp, "w"))
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/state/{repo}", exist_ok=True)
        open(f"{th}/.cc/state/{repo}/paused", "w").write(PAUSED_FLAG)
        o0, a1 = offered(), alerted()
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: a parked project's row is neither offered to its session nor paged about — the owner "
           "parked it himself, so a nag about it is spend he asked the box to stop",
           offered() == o0 and alerted() == a1)
        ok("end to end: …and no nag memo is written for it either, so the finding is news again at the resume "
           "with its `unlanded for Nh` clock counting from then instead of through the park",
           json.load(open(seenp)) == {})
        ok("end to end: …while the reconciling itself still runs for it: the row is in the snapshot exactly as "
           "before, because a pause stops what SPENDS, not what keeps the board true",
           (snap_().get(f"{repo}/t2") or {}).get("state"))
        os.remove(f"{th}/.cc/state/{repo}/paused")
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: …and that same row, resumed, is offered once — the pause was what silenced it and the "
           "finding was not lost", offered() == o0 + 1)
        # per project, never box-wide: park a DIFFERENT project and this one is nagged exactly as before
        json.dump({}, open(seenp, "w"))
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/state/{repo}-other", exist_ok=True)
        open(f"{th}/.cc/state/{repo}-other/paused", "w").write(PAUSED_FLAG)
        o1 = offered()
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: parking ANOTHER project does not silence this one — the gate asks about the repo each row "
           "belongs to, never whether something somewhere is parked", offered() == o1 + 1)
        shutil.rmtree(f"{th}/.cc/state/{repo}-other")
        json.dump({}, open(seenp, "w"))

        # A ROW SOMEBODY IS DELIBERATELY SITTING ON is skipped the same way, and THIS is the line that made it
        # worth a state: "review and land it" went out four times in one day about rows people were holding, once
        # about a PR whose approval had been withdrawn because landing it early opened a hole. Own fixture, clean
        # ledger and the real cc-board writing the word, so nothing but the hold can be the reason.
        o2, a2 = offered(), alerted()
        cb("set", repo, "t2", "held", "behind #309 — landing this one first opens the hole")
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: a held row is never offered as 'review and land it', is not paged about, and no memo is "
           "kept — so the PR is news again, once, when a person lifts the hold",
           offered() == o2 and alerted() == a2
           and not any("t2" in k for k in json.load(open(seenp))))
        ok("end to end: …and --apply leaves the row itself exactly as it was — its word, its PR and its reason "
           "all stand, because a held row is one a person still has to be able to see and release",
           cb("get", repo, "t2", "status").stdout.strip() == "review"
           and cb("get", repo, "t2", "pr").stdout.strip() == "https://example.invalid/pr/9"
           and cb("get", repo, "t2", "held").stdout.strip().startswith("behind #309"))
        ok("end to end: …and `show` still lists it with that reason: this view is the only place a held row is "
           "offered to a person at all", "held (" in cb("show", repo).stdout
           and "behind #309" in cb("show", repo).stdout)
        cb("set", repo, "t2", "held", "")
        subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("end to end: …and that same row, hold lifted, is offered once — the hold was what silenced it and the "
           "finding was not lost", offered() == o2 + 1)
        json.dump({}, open(seenp, "w"))

        # A PR THE BOX HAS ALREADY READ AND REFUSED IS NOT ONE TO "REVIEW AND LAND". PR #404 was offered to its
        # session at 16:3xZ on 2026-09-11 with DO-NOT-LAND recorded against its head twice — the landing's own
        # re-review and a security read — while a replacement row was already building the fix. `OPEN` was all
        # this rule ever read, it cost a wake-up and a turn to say no, and a less careful seat would have queued a
        # refused change for deploy. Own fixture, clean ledger, and a `gh` that answers the verdict question the
        # way GitHub does, so nothing but the verdict can be the reason.
        vj, HD = f"{th}/verdict.json", "09dd584c561e9e18d3025a8db244809f9d5b776a"
        open(f"{th}/gh", "w").write(f'#!/bin/sh\ncase "$*" in\n  *headRefOid*) cat {vj} ;;\n  *) echo OPEN ;;\nesac\n')
        os.chmod(f"{th}/gh", 0o755)
        mark = f"<!-- cc-land-review v2 change=369953f4b8fd base=a178c4925378 head={HD[:12]} verdict=%s by=w -->"

        def verdicts(*bodies):
            """Offers made in one run with exactly these comments on the PR, off a clean ledger."""
            json.dump({"headRefOid": HD, "comments": [{"body": b} for b in bodies]}, open(vj, "w"))
            json.dump({}, open(seenp, "w"))
            o_ = offered()
            subprocess.run(argvP, env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
            return offered() - o_

        ok("end to end: a PR with DO-NOT-LAND recorded against the head it is at NOW is never offered as 'review "
           "and land it' — the box read this change and said no, and the PR state is not the verdict",
           verdicts(mark % "DO-NOT-LAND" + "\n1. a.py:1 — the finding") == 0)
        ok("end to end: …and no memo is kept for it, so the PR is news again, once, when the head moves past the "
           "verdict", not any("t2" in k for k in json.load(open(seenp))))
        ok("end to end: LAND-AFTER-FIX stops a landing exactly as DO-NOT-LAND does, so 'review and land it' is "
           "the wrong move for it too — the remedy is the fix that verdict names",
           verdicts(mark % "LAND-AFTER-FIX") == 0)
        ok("end to end: the older head-keyed marker shape is read the same way — a verdict recorded before the "
           "change key existed is still a refusal",
           verdicts(f"<!-- cc-land-review {HD[:12]} DO-NOT-LAND -->") == 0)
        ok("end to end: a verdict about a head the PR has moved PAST is spent: the push answered it, the change "
           "is unread again and landing it is the right offer",
           verdicts("<!-- cc-land-review v2 change=1 base=2 head=ffffffffffff verdict=DO-NOT-LAND by=w -->") == 1)
        ok("end to end: a LAND standing on this head silences nothing — that is the watchdog's line being right",
           verdicts(mark % "LAND") == 1)
        ok("end to end: …and a finding that QUOTES a marker is text about the change, not the answer to it: the "
           "read is anchored at the start of the comment, as cc-land's own is",
           verdicts("1. cc-land:9 — a comment that starts " + mark % "DO-NOT-LAND") == 1)
        open(f"{th}/gh", "w").write("#!/bin/sh\necho OPEN\n")
        os.chmod(f"{th}/gh", 0o755)
        json.dump({}, open(seenp, "w"))

        victim = subprocess.Popen(["sleep", "60"], start_new_session=True)   # its own group, so nothing else is in range
        ended = end_it([victim.pid])
        try:
            victim.wait(timeout=10)
            died = True
        except Exception:
            victim.kill()
            died = False
        ok("ending a runaway really ends it, group and all", ended and died)
        ok("a process that is already gone counts as ended, not as a failure", end_it([victim.pid]))
        # A FIXTURE IS NOT A BOARD: a `_cctest<pid>` board — another suite's, ALIVE (a sleeper stands in for it: a
        # sibling, not an ancestor) — beside the real one is neither reconciled nor in the snapshot cc-digest and the
        # Home tab read; the sweep the unfiltered pass gives cc-janitor leaves it (its pid lives); and one whose <pid>
        # is THIS pass's ancestor is the suite's own — reconciled like any board (the suite reads its own track)
        other = subprocess.Popen(["sleep", "300"])
        fx, own = f"_cctest{other.pid}", f"_cctest{os.getpid()}"
        try:
            for r in (fx, own):
                cb("init", r, f"{th}/dev/{r}", "main")
                cb("add", r, "w1", "push refused", "")
                cb("status", r, "w1", "waiting")
            for w in ("w1", "w5"):                      # the worktrees a suite lays down: one on its board, one not
                os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/{fx}/{w}", exist_ok=True)
            rF = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "--apply", "--no-net", "--grace", "0"],
                                env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        finally:
            other.kill(); other.wait()
        snpF = json.load(open(f"{th}/.cc/state/reconcile.json"))["tracks"]
        ok("a `_cctest<pid>` board is a selftest fixture, not a board: the unfiltered pass reconciles the real repo "
           "beside it and never it — not in the output, not in the snapshot the digest and the Home tab read",
           rF.returncode in (0, 1) and fx not in rF.stdout and not any(k.startswith(fx) for k in snpF)
           and any(k.startswith(repo) for k in snpF))
        ok("…and its WORKTREES are that suite's scaffolding too, never 'a worktree with no board entry': the run "
           "prints every record, and neither the one on the fixture board nor the one with no row at all is there",
           f"{fx}/w1" not in rF.stdout and f"{fx}/w5" not in rF.stdout)
        ok("…and a fixture whose suite is alive survives the sweep that pass gives cc-janitor",
           os.path.exists(f"{th}/.cc/boards/{fx}.json"))
        ok("…while a `_cctest<pid>` board whose <pid> is this pass's own ancestor is the suite's own: reconciled and "
           "snapshotted like any board — the suite's `cc ls` and `cc digest` must see their track",
           any(k.startswith(own) for k in snpF))
        # …AND THE PREFIX IS THE NAMESPACE, NOT ONE SUITE'S NAME. cc-sandbox's selfchecks lay down `_ccsbx<pid>` — a
        # ~/dev clone and worktrees, no board of its own at all — and a leaked one was reported here as two worktrees
        # of work no board knew (2026-09-02). Its own fixtures, both halves in the one pass.
        for w in ("t", "u"):
            os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/_ccsbx99999999/{w}", exist_ok=True)
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/{repo}/stray", exist_ok=True)
        rS = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "--no-net", "--grace", "0"],
                            env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("a leaked `_ccsbx<pid>` is a suite's scratch on the same rule as `_cctest<pid>`, so its worktrees raise "
           "nothing — while one under a REAL repo with no row on its board is still work the board never heard of",
           "_ccsbx99999999" not in rS.stdout
           and f"{repo}/stray" in rS.stdout and "a worktree with no board entry" in rS.stdout)
        shutil.rmtree(f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/{repo}/stray", ignore_errors=True)
        shutil.rmtree(f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/_ccsbx99999999", ignore_errors=True)
        # A DELIVERY IN FLIGHT IS NOT A WORKTREE THE BOARD NEVER HEARD OF (2026-09-11, twice: PR #425 open and
        # PR #429 queued, both reported, both a wasted seat wake-up). A real checkout on a real branch, `gh` faked
        # on PATH the way the pending cases fake it, and the PR list swapped per case — so what is pinned is the
        # rule and not the fixture. The control comes FIRST: with no PR at all the worktree is still reported.
        deliv, prj = f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/{repo}/deliver", f"{th}/prs.json"
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/dev/{repo}", exist_ok=True)      # branch_prs asks from the repo's own checkout or not at all
        subprocess.run(["git", "init", "-q", "-b", "track/deliver-x", deliv], capture_output=True, timeout=30)
        open(f"{th}/gh", "w").write(f'#!/bin/sh\ncase "$*" in\n  *"pr list"*) cat {prj} ;;\n  *) echo OPEN ;;\nesac\n')
        os.chmod(f"{th}/gh", 0o755)

        def orphaned(*flags):
            open(prj, "w").write(flags[0])
            r_ = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), repo, *flags[1:]],
                                env=penv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
            return f"{repo}/deliver" in r_.stdout

        ok("a worktree whose branch has no PR and no landing is work the board never heard of, as it always was",
           orphaned("[]", "--grace", "0"))
        ok("…but one whose branch has an OPEN PR is a delivery in flight — the builder's worktree is named for its "
           "branch, so no row will ever carry that name, and the offer is a wake-up that finds nothing to do",
           not orphaned('[{"number":9,"state":"OPEN"}]', "--grace", "0"))
        ok("…and one whose PR MERGED a minute ago is that same delivery landing: the merge, the worktree's removal "
           "and this pass race each other, and a person was paged about a worktree whose PR had merged 106 s "
           "before the run (2026-09-11)",
           not orphaned('[{"number":9,"state":"MERGED","mergedAt":"%s"}]'
                        % time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(now() - 60)), "--grace", "0"))
        ok("…while one merged longer ago than a landing's own gap is a leftover nothing is coming back for, and "
           "is reported as a worktree with no board entry always was",
           orphaned('[{"number":9,"state":"MERGED","mergedAt":"%s"}]'
                    % time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(now() - BETWEEN_ROUNDS - 60)),
                    "--grace", "0"))
        ok("…while a PR that is closed and nobody is landing leaves the worktree a leftover again: reported",
           orphaned('[{"number":9,"state":"CLOSED"}]', "--grace", "0"))
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/state/land", exist_ok=True)
        json.dump({"repo": repo, "pr": 9}, open(f"{th}/.cc/state/land/{repo}-9.json", "w"))
        ok("…and the same PR with a job in the landing queue is mid-delivery, whatever state GitHub says it is in",
           not orphaned('[{"number":9,"state":"CLOSED"}]', "--grace", "0"))
        os.remove(f"{th}/.cc/state/land/{repo}-9.json")
        ok("a worktree made inside the grace window is not reported before its row or its PR can exist",
           not orphaned("[]"))
        ok("…and with no network the branch cannot be looked up, so nothing is forgiven on suspicion",
           orphaned('[{"number":9,"state":"OPEN"}]', "--grace", "0", "--no-net"))
        # the board's OWN answer, no network needed: a row that names this directory is a board entry for it
        cb("add", repo, "namedwt", "a row whose worktree is not named after it", "")
        cb("set", repo, "namedwt", "worktree", deliv)
        ok("a row that names the directory outright is the board having heard of it, under any row name and with "
           "no GitHub in it at all", not orphaned("[]", "--grace", "0", "--no-net"))
        shutil.rmtree(deliv, ignore_errors=True)
    # ONE FIXTURE, EVERY CONSUMER (arch review 2026-09-08, rec 8). Five tracks the box disagrees with its board about —
    # a stale board, a blocked journal, a live worker, an idle pane, an agent mark — decided here once, and the Home
    # tab and cc-pulse must read the same word, liveness, who and why off that snapshot: both are imported as modules
    # and handed the rows the way home_snapshot() and Run.snapshot() hand them, so neither has a ladder of its own left
    # to disagree with. `cc ls` and cc digest take `view`, which is this same dict on disk. No process and no tmux: the
    # facts ARE the fixture, exactly as in the decision table above.
    def load(name):
        import importlib.machinery, importlib.util
        sp = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(name.replace("-", "_"),
                                             importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(name.replace("-", "_"), f"{BIN}/{name}"))
        m = importlib.util.module_from_spec(sp); sp.loader.exec_module(m)
        return m
    five = {"stale": F(status="blocked", live=True, workers=[1]),              # the board says blocked; a worker is in it
            "asked": F(status="running", journal=("BLOCKED", "which key?")),    # its journal declared a question
            "worker": F(status="running", live=True, workers=[1], loops=[2]),   # a loop and its worker, working
            "idle": F(status="running", live=True, pane_idle=3 * 3600),         # a pane open, nobody at it for 3 h
            "marked": F(status="queued", agent="@3:900", agent_live=True)}      # a live session's subagent has it
    rows5 = []
    for t, fx5 in five.items():
        st5 = effective(fx5, decide(fx5))
        rows5.append(("r", t, fx5, st5) + (waits_for(fx5) if st5 not in TERMINAL else ("", "")))
    snap5 = snapshot(rows5)["tracks"]
    ok("the fixture decides as the table says — running · waiting · running · idle · running, alive where something "
       "is, the question and the mark and the loop and the idle clock carried",
       {k: (v["state"], v["live"], v["waiting_on"]) for k, v in snap5.items()}
       == {"r/stale": ("running", True, ""), "r/asked": ("waiting", False, "person"), "r/worker": ("running", True, ""),
           "r/idle": ("idle", True, ""), "r/marked": ("running", True, "")}
       and snap5["r/asked"]["why"] == "which key?" and snap5["r/marked"]["agent"] and snap5["r/worker"]["loop"]
       and snap5["r/idle"]["idle_for"] == 3 * 3600)
    th5 = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cc-reconcile-consumers-")
    try:
        os.makedirs(f"{th5}/.cc/boards")
        board5 = {t: {"status": fx5["status"], "updated": "2026-09-01T00:00:00Z"} for t, fx5 in five.items()}
        json.dump({"repo": "r", "tracks": board5}, open(f"{th5}/.cc/boards/r.json", "w"))
        slack, pulse = load("cc-slack"), load("cc-pulse")
        slack.HOME = th5
        home5 = {t["name"]: t for t in slack.home_tracks(snap5, {})[0]}
        ok("THE HOME TAB SAYS WHAT THIS SAID — the same word and the same question for all five, the board it "
           "contradicts printed as stale, the idle clock and no iteration count where no loop is: it reads "
           "home_snapshot() and decides nothing of its own",
           {k: v["state"] for k, v in home5.items()} == {k: v["state"] for k, v in snap5.items()}
           and {k: v["reason"] for k, v in home5.items()}
               == {k: (v["why"] if v["waiting_on"] == "person" else "") for k, v in snap5.items()}
           and "board stale" in home5["r/stale"]["detail"] and "board stale" in home5["r/marked"]["detail"]
           and "idle 3h" in home5["r/idle"]["detail"] and "board stale" not in home5["r/worker"]["detail"])
        pw5 = pulse.words("r", board5, snap5)
        ok("AND cc-pulse SAYS THE SAME — the word each row wears for its tick is this snapshot's, and the marked row is "
           "the one a subagent has: it reads Run.snapshot() and lists no tmux and no ps of its own",
           {k: w for k, (w, _, _) in pw5.items()} == {k.split("/", 1)[1]: v["state"] for k, v in snap5.items()}
           and {k for k, (a, _) in ((k, (a, h)) for k, (_, a, h) in pw5.items()) if a} == {"marked"}
           and not any(h for _, _, h in pw5.values()))
        home0 = {t["name"]: t for t in slack.home_tracks({}, {})[0]}
        pw0 = pulse.words("r", board5, None)
        ok("…and with NO snapshot both fall back the way `view` does — the board's own word, nothing alive, no question, "
           "no mark holding — so a decider that cannot answer never splits the two",
           {k: (v["state"], v["reason"], v["detail"]) for k, v in home0.items()}
           == {f"r/{t}": (r["status"], "", "—") for t, r in board5.items()}
           and pw0 == {t: (r["status"], False, False) for t, r in board5.items()})
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(th5, ignore_errors=True)
    # A PIPE PLANTED WHERE A BOARD SHOULD BE (review of #224): the read is cc-board's opener now, so it is no board, never a hang
    def bounded(fn, secs=10):   # a read that hangs on the pipe (a plain open would, for good) fails THIS case, not the suite
        import threading
        box = []
        def go():
            try: box.append(fn())
            except Exception as e: box.append(e)
        t = threading.Thread(target=go, daemon=True); t.start(); t.join(secs)
        return box[0] if box else "hung"
    _b, BOARDS = BOARDS, tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cc-reconcile-pipe-")
    try:
        os.mkfifo(f"{BOARDS}/pipe.json")
        json.dump({"repo": "real", "tracks": {}}, open(f"{BOARDS}/real.json", "w"))
        ok("boards(): a pipe planted where a board should be is unreadable (None), not a hang, and the real one beside it reads",
           bounded(lambda: [(os.path.basename(fn), d) for fn, d in boards()]) == [("pipe.json", None), ("real.json", {"repo": "real", "tracks": {}})])
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(BOARDS, ignore_errors=True); BOARDS = _b
    # ── the failure investigation: inert until the owner's setting, held by the tier, one claim, one launch ──
    th6 = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cc-reconcile-inv-")
    try:
        env6 = dict(os.environ, HOME=th6, CC_CONFIG=f"{th6}/.cc/config", CC_FAILURES=f"{th6}/.cc/failures",
                    CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY="1", CC_NOTIFY_LOG=f"{th6}/notify.log", CC_RECONCILE_INJECT="/bin/true",
                    CC_RECONCILE_LAUNCH=f"{th6}/launch")
        os.makedirs(f"{th6}/.cc/boards"); os.makedirs(f"{th6}/.cc/state")
        open(f"{th6}/launch", "w").write(f'#!/bin/sh\necho "$@" >> {th6}/launch.log\n'); os.chmod(f"{th6}/launch", 0o755)
        F6 = f"{BIN}/cc-failures"

        def rec6(kind, days_ago, **kw):
            ts = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(time.time() - days_ago * 86400))
            a = [F6, "record", "--scope", "sc", "--kind", kind, "--observed", "o", "--ts", ts]
            for k, v in kw.items():
                a += [f"--{k}", v]
            subprocess.run(a, env=env6, capture_output=True)

        def launched():
            return open(f"{th6}/launch.log").read().splitlines() if os.path.exists(f"{th6}/launch.log") else []

        def elig():
            return subprocess.run([F6, "eligible"], env=env6, capture_output=True, text=True).stdout

        def run6(*extra):
            return subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), *extra, "--no-net", "--grace", "0"],
                                  env=env6, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)

        for d in (5, 3, 1):
            rec6("guard-policy/permitted-text-refused", d)           # three episodes: eligible on the rule
        r = run6("--apply", "--notify")
        ok("INERT: with no setting the pass claims nothing and launches nothing; the cluster reads eligible and unclaimed",
           launched() == [] and "unclaimed" in elig() and "investigation" not in r.stdout)
        subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-config", "set", "CC_FAILURES_INVESTIGATE", "on"], env=env6, capture_output=True)
        # the tier is pinned in the ENVIRONMENT, not written with `cc-tier set`: the environment outranks the file
        # (cc-config), and selftest.sh exports CC_SPEND_TIER=autonomous over every fixture it runs
        env6["CC_SPEND_TIER"] = "moderate"
        r = run6("--apply", "--notify")
        ok("TIER STAND-DOWN: the setting on but `cc-tier allows explore` says no (moderate): no claim, no launch, "
           "the cluster still eligible and unclaimed with its age, and the run says the tier holds it",
           launched() == [] and re.search(r"\tunclaimed \d", elig()) and "tier holds" in r.stdout)
        env6["CC_SPEND_TIER"] = "autonomous"
        # a `cc-audit failure` running on the BOX (here: a sleeping script by that name) must not hold a stubbed pass —
        # the running-investigation scan is box-wide, and skipped under CC_RECONCILE_LAUNCH
        open(f"{th6}/cc-audit", "w").write("#!/bin/sh\nsleep 60\n"); os.chmod(f"{th6}/cc-audit", 0o755)
        ghost = subprocess.Popen([f"{th6}/cc-audit", "failure", "i-elsewhere"], stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        try:
            r = run6("--apply", "--notify")
        finally:
            ghost.kill(); ghost.wait()
        ok("LAUNCH: the setting on and the tier autonomous: ONE claim, ONE launch of `cc-audit failure <claim>` — "
           "with a `cc-audit failure` of the box's own running, which a stubbed pass does not see",
           len(launched()) == 1 and launched()[0].startswith("failure i-") and "claimed" in elig() and "investigation" in r.stdout)
        r = run6("--apply", "--notify")
        ok("CLAIM ONCE: a second pass launches nothing — the cluster is held by its claim", len(launched()) == 1)
        ok("…and the launch is in reconcile.log", os.path.exists(f"{th6}/.cc/state/reconcile.log")
           and "investigation" in open(f"{th6}/.cc/state/reconcile.log").read())
        rec6("gate-target/wrong-revision", 1, critical="main-gate-or-deploy-red")   # a second cluster, critical: the day's other slot
        r = run6()
        ok("a report-only pass (no --apply) never launches, whatever is eligible", len(launched()) == 1)
        r = run6("snapshot")
        ok("…nor does `snapshot`, which is pure", len(launched()) == 1)
        r = run6("--apply", "--notify")
        ok("…the next --apply pass claims and launches the critical cluster: one launch per pass", len(launched()) == 2 and "failure i-" in launched()[1])
        notified = open(f"{th6}/notify.log").read() if os.path.exists(f"{th6}/notify.log") else ""   # the handoff sweep may log the box's own panes here
        ok("no page and no board row came of it: a claim and a launch are ledger writes, and the report is cc-audit's to deliver",
           "investigation" not in notified and all(ln.split()[1] not in notified for ln in launched()) and os.listdir(f"{th6}/.cc/boards") == [])
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(th6, ignore_errors=True)
    print(f"cc-reconcile selfcheck: {p} passed, {f} failed")
    return 1 if f else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(130)
